//! Source-bound x86-64 body theorem (slices 1 to 21).
//!
//! One theorem per function states that the ABI-observable behaviour of the
//! exact x86-64 instruction stream the certificate lane verifies (the
//! pre-register-allocation `X86ISelFunction`) equals that of its exact LIR
//! source, for every input, every incoming memory and every callee
//! behaviour:
//!
//! ```text
//! inputs      e_R   entry value of each read-only root: the first n System V
//!                   argument registers, RBX RBP R12-R15 and RSP (64 bits)
//!             r_c   result of the c-th direct call, at its LIR width (q_c:
//!                   its second result lane, in RDX)
//!             g_c   bits of RAX above that result, which the call leaves
//!                   undefined (declared only where the machine reads them;
//!                   h_c: the bits of RDX above a second lane)
//!             l_j   bytes the j-th paired incoming-memory load reads, at its
//!                   memory width (a bool load reads one whole byte)
//!             sym_j address of the j-th symbol the source references, per
//!                   form of reference (see Symbols)
//!             u_k   k-th undefined machine value the (psABI) walk reads: the
//!                   bits above a narrow load's byte or word or above a
//!                   `setcc` byte (w_k in the clean-bool walk)
//!             f_k   initial value of the k-th private stack byte read before
//!                   any store wrote it, on either side (slice 6)
//!             d     `d{c}s{x}o{y}`: the byte call site c leaves at offset y
//!                   of private slot x, whose address escaped (slice 7)
//!                   (slice 8: `r_c`/`q_c` of an aggregate call result are
//!                   its eightbytes, as wide as their bytes; an aggregate
//!                   parameter's bytes are bytes of its argument registers'
//!                   entry values e_R)
//!             p_i   source parameter i (LIR Value(i)), at its LIR width
//!             a_i   psABI view of a B1 parameter's register (see below)
//!             z_i   clean-bool view of a B1 parameter's register (see below)
//!             s_k   k-th source result, in flattened chain order, LIR width
//!             m_k   64-bit value written by the k-th machine write
//!             n_k   the same write under the clean-bool view (see below)
//! premises    p_i = low W_i bits of e_arg(i)           verify-owned System V table
//! (each one   a_i = e_arg(i) with bits 1..7 cleared      psABI _Bool rule
//!  defines a  z_i = e_arg(i) with bits 1..31 cleared     the backend's clean bool
//!  fresh      s_k = f_LIR(...), a call result is r_c,    exact LIR semantics
//!  name)            a load is l_j (a bool load l_j != 0)
//!                   or private-slot bytes
//!             m_k, n_k = f_x86(current operand roots)    exact x86 semantics
//! source      each returned LIR value (one, or a RAX:RDX pair), widened to
//!             its lane's observed return bits, then the world trace in
//!             order: every call's LIR arguments at the bits the callee
//!             reads (then, slice 7, every escaped slot's contents at the
//!             call), every incoming access's address and every incoming
//!             store's value at its memory width
//! machine     the observed return bits of RAX (and RDX) at the RET, then the
//!             same trace: every call's argument registers at the CALL (and
//!             the escaped slots' bytes there), every paired load/store
//!             row's address and stored bytes
//! theorem     one observed item: (= source machine); several items: the
//!             conjunction of their per-item equations, stated against
//!             `true`: (= true (and (= s_1 m_1) (and ... (= s_n m_n))));
//!             several source paths (slice 6): one claim per path, below
//! ```
//!
//! **Instruction coverage (slice 10).** Six more LIR operations, each with
//! trust-ir's exact meaning (`trust_ir::interpret`), and the machine rows the
//! x86-64 selector lowers them to, each with its Intel SDM meaning:
//!
//! - *Checked arithmetic* (`CheckedUadd`, `CheckedUsub`, `CheckedUmul`,
//!   `CheckedSadd`, `CheckedSsub`, `CheckedSmul` of two `I64` operands, the
//!   adapter's lowering of a 64-bit trust-ir `Overflow`; a narrower one is
//!   ordinary arithmetic and compares in LIR): the results `[value, overflow]` are the
//!   wrapping sum, difference or product, and the `bool` that the
//!   mathematical result is outside the carrier's unsigned or signed range
//!   (`eval_int_overflow`), computed exactly at a width it cannot overflow
//!   ([`CheckedOp::overflows`]). The selector computes the value with
//!   `add`/`sub`/`imul` and the overflow with `setb` (unsigned add and
//!   subtract: CF) or `seto` (signed: OF), or with `mov rax, a; mul b;
//!   setb`. The walk models the flags of exactly those rows ([`AluFlags`]):
//!   `add` and `sub` define every status flag (`sub` as `cmp`; `add` with
//!   CF the carry out of the top bit, `x86_64_eflags::encode_add_eflags`);
//!   `imul` defines CF = OF = "the sign extension of the truncated product
//!   differs from the full product" and `mul` CF = OF = "the high half is not
//!   zero", leaving SF, ZF, AF and PF undefined, so a reader of one of those
//!   is an [`X86BodyMismatch::UndefinedRead`] of `rflags`. `mul src` reads
//!   EAX/RAX and writes EDX:EAX/RDX:RAX at the width `src` names (each 32-bit
//!   half zero-extended). The flags still never cross a block boundary, every
//!   other ALU row still leaves them unmodelled, and an `add` or `sub` of a
//!   private stack address writes none the walk models.
//! - *`Bnot`* of an integer (trust-ir `Not`, `!raw` at the width; trust-ir
//!   has no `not` of a `bool`, which stays outside) and the `not dst, src`
//!   row (complement at the operand width); likewise *`Ineg`* (trust-ir
//!   `Neg`, `0 - raw` wrapping) and `neg dst, src`.
//! - *`CtPop`* (trust-ir `CtPop`, `count_ones` at the width, a result of the
//!   operand's carrier) and the `popcnt dst, src` row; for an `I8`/`I16`
//!   operand the selector first masks a copy with the tied `and d, imm`
//!   (admitted as `d = d & imm`, like slice 8's tied rows). On a target
//!   without POPCNT (the Darwin x86-64 baseline), the pipeline expands the
//!   `popcnt` row into the generic SWAR sequence after this theorem's
//!   subject (`expand_x86_generic_popcnts`), like the register allocation
//!   and the other post-selection rewrites: that expansion is outside this
//!   theorem, checked by its own pass validation
//!   (`validate_x86_popcnt_expansion_canary`: widths 8 and 32 on every
//!   compile, width 64 only by a dedicated proof test).
//! - *Variable shifts* (`Ishl`, `Ushr`, `Sshr` by an amount of the value's
//!   carrier that is no constant of the chain) and `shl`/`shr`/`sar dst, src`
//!   by CL, the low byte of RCX masked to 5 or 6 bits (the selector moves the
//!   amount into ECX/RCX, masking a narrow one to its width first, and
//!   extends a narrow operand of `shr`/`sar`). For an amount below the width
//!   the source is trust-ir's shift. At the width or above trust-ir has
//!   undefined behavior (`interpret::shift_amount`), which any value refines,
//!   and the theorem states the source as the shift by the amount modulo the
//!   width there ([`SourceOp::VariableShift`]): one refinement of the
//!   undefined behavior, chosen so that the theorem holds for the selector's
//!   masked lowering wherever the source is defined, and it is never weaker
//!   than trust-ir at an in-range amount. A constant amount stays slice 1's
//!   in-range rule (an out-of-range constant stays outside).
//! - *`CallIndirect`* (`args[0]` an `I64` function pointer, the rest the
//!   call's arguments) and `call r64`: slice 3's Ackermann-reduced call model
//!   with the callee pointer as one more argument of the uninterpreted
//!   callee. The walk pairs the k-th machine call with the k-th source call
//!   (an indirect one only with `call r64`, a direct one only with `call
//!   symbol`, else [`X86BodyMismatch::CallTarget`]), and the theorem
//!   observes the call register's 64 bits at the call against the source's
//!   function pointer, before its arguments: a call through another value
//!   is refuted. The callee contract is slice 3's (and slice 7's for escaped
//!   slots), whatever function the pointer names; an indirect return-buffer
//!   callee stays outside.
//!
//! **Floating-point operations and the remaining atomics (slice 21).**
//! Rulings OD-198 to OD-201 in `docs/decision-record.md`.
//!
//! - *Rounding operations are paired events* (OD-198, which supersedes
//!   OD-73 for them). LIR `Fadd`, `Fsub`, `Fmul` and `Fdiv` of two `F32` or
//!   `F64` patterns, `FcvtFromInt`/`FcvtFromUint` of an `I64` to an `F32` or
//!   `F64`, and the in-range value of `FcvtToInt` of an `F64` to an `I64` are
//!   EVENTS of the world trace ([`SourceFloat`]), like calls. The machine row
//!   that computes the same IEEE 754 operation ([`machine_float_key`]:
//!   `addsd`..`divss`, `cvtsi2sd`/`cvtsi2ss r64`, `cvttsd2si`, `cvtsd2si`)
//!   pairs with the source's next event when their operator keys agree
//!   (operation, format, rounding direction; [`FloatKey`]) and its TV-1 stamp
//!   names the event; the theorem then OBSERVES the row's operands against
//!   the event's (items, as a call's arguments) and both sides read the
//!   result as one free input, the event's FP leaf `fe{k}`
//!   ([`float_event_name`]). A rounding row no event pairs gets an FP leaf
//!   of its own (`fu{k}`/`fw{k}`), and the theorem holds for every value of
//!   it. Neither side ever states what an operation computes, so the
//!   theorem claims refinement: the run in which each paired trust-ir
//!   operation returns what the machine computed is one trust-ir permits,
//!   because trust-ir's operation is the IEEE 754 one at the operand's
//!   format, rounding to nearest even, with ANY NaN result (`docs/
//!   ub-numerics-policy.md` §2), and the machine's is the same operation
//!   with one particular NaN (trusted: the Intel SDM transcription and the
//!   default MXCSR, `0x1F80`). No NaN payload is ever claimed (OD-112's
//!   line). `FcvtFromUint` pairs with a signed conversion only with its
//!   operand's bit 63 claimed clear (one more item), where the signed and
//!   unsigned readings of the word are one integer. A narrower conversion,
//!   `Fsqrt`, `Fma`, `Fmin`/`Fmax`, rounding to an integer, `FPExt`,
//!   `FPTrunc` and `FcvtToUint` stay outside by name.
//! - *Compares are exact.* LIR `Fcmp { cond }` and the flags of `ucomisd`/
//!   `ucomiss` (ZF, PF, CF; OF and SF cleared) are stated over the bits with
//!   the FP-to-BV lowering's predicates ([`FloatRelation`]: `nan`, the
//!   order `key`, `ordered`); `setcc`, `cmovcc` and `jcc` read the flags
//!   through the shared condition evaluation.
//! - *The float-to-integer conversion saturates* (OD-199). LIR `FcvtToInt {
//!   dst_ty: I64 }` translates both trust-ir `FPToSI` (undefined out of
//!   range) and `FPToSISat`, so its meaning is the stronger, saturating one
//!   ([`saturating_f64_to_i64`]); `cvttsd2si` returns the integer
//!   indefinite where the value does not fit ([`f64_fits_i64`]).
//! - *An abstract theorem's satisfying model is no counterexample* (OD-200).
//!   A theorem with a rounding event or row
//!   ([`SourceBoundX86Body::is_fp_abstract`]) abstracts its rounding
//!   operations (its pairings claim operand patterns equal, its results are
//!   FP leaves), so the verifier leaves its rows uncredited, never failed,
//!   when AY satisfies its negation.
//! - *Atomics* (OD-201, OD-83's single-threaded claim). An `I128`
//!   `AtomicLoad` (any load ordering, a non-private address) pairs only with
//!   the selector's `AtomicLoadCmpxchg16b [m]` row, which is its two 8-byte
//!   halves (RAX the low, RDX the high), RCX and RBX zero, memory unchanged
//!   (both branches of `lock cmpxchg16b` with RDX:RAX = RCX:RBX = 0). Its
//!   write of the callee-saved RBX is a clobber the frame restores
//!   (trusted, OD-201), and the 16 bytes are taken to be writable (the
//!   pseudo writes them back; Rust makes a 16-byte atomic load of read-only
//!   memory undefined behaviour; trusted, OD-201). An `AtomicRmw { op: Xchg
//!   }` of an `I32`/`I64` (any ordering) is an incoming load of the old
//!   bytes and a store of the operand at the same address, paired only with
//!   the selector's `AtomicRmwCasLoop [dst, src, [m], 5]` row (`dst` and RAX
//!   the old bytes, R10 the operand; with one thread its loop runs once).
//!   Every other read-modify-write, compare-exchange, fence and an `I128`
//!   atomic store stay outside.
//!
//! **The `I128` division class (slice 22).** Ruling OD-202 in
//! `docs/decision-record.md`, amended by OD-210 and OD-211 (the hostile
//! review of slice 22), OD-216 (the link unit's definitions) and OD-217
//! (the C memory routines are guarded alike).
//!
//! - *An `I128` division is the call of its runtime routine.* x86-64 has no
//!   128-bit `div`: the selector lowers an `I128` `Udiv`, `Sdiv`, `Urem` or
//!   `Srem` to a call of `__udivti3`, `__divti3`, `__umodti3` or `__modti3`
//!   by symbol (`select_i128_div_libcall`), and the theorem states the LIR
//!   division as exactly that call, under the call model of slices 3, 7 and
//!   13 ([`i128_division_routine`]): its operands are the call's two `I128`
//!   arguments (RDI:RSI, RDX:RCX), observed against the machine's argument
//!   registers, and its result is the call's `I128` result (RAX:RDX), one
//!   free input both sides share. CHECKED: the argument placement, the
//!   symbol each opcode calls, the result's use and every other call fact
//!   ([`check_call_row`]). TRUSTED (the routine contract, the same kind as
//!   slice 13's `memcpy`): that the routine the link binds to the name (for
//!   a Rust program `compiler_builtins`', for a C link the platform's
//!   compiler-rt, or a definition in another module of the link) returns
//!   trust-ir's `udiv`/`sdiv`/`urem`/`srem` of its arguments on trust-ir's
//!   defined domain (a nonzero divisor; for the signed pair, not `MIN /
//!   -1`). OD-211 (amending OD-202): it also writes no memory the caller
//!   observes (no escaped slot, no incoming memory), has no effect on the
//!   world, and returns. That is MORE than the call model assumes of a
//!   callee (slices 3 and 7 let one rewrite the escaped slots and the world,
//!   or not return): it is what links the call to trust-ir's pure division.
//!   No undefined stop is stated: the call is total, and off the defined
//!   domain trust-ir owes nothing.
//! - *The routine is never the module's own* (OD-210). A routine the module
//!   under proof defines, or the function under proof itself, is what the
//!   call binds to, unchecked: such a theorem is refused
//!   ([`X86ModuleDefinitions`], [`X86BodyUnsupported::TrustedRoutine`]), as
//!   is every one whose caller names no module. OD-216: nor its link unit's.
//!   The module is one compile unit (for the rustc bridge a codegen unit, or
//!   one function of one), so a definition in a sibling unit of the same
//!   crate binds unseen: the compiler unions the definitions the frontend
//!   names for its link unit (`CompilerConfig::link_unit_definitions`;
//!   [`X86ModuleDefinitions::with_link_unit`]), and such a routine is
//!   refused too ([`X86RoutineDefinition::TheLinkUnit`]).
//! - *An `I128` divide-by-zero guard* is slice 14's guard at 128 bits: the
//!   selector's carrier is `or d, lo, hi; TrapDivZeroExact d`, walked
//!   exactly; a guard of a nonzero 128-bit constant never fires.
//!
//! **Arithmetic congruence pairing and the generalization over arithmetic
//! leaves (slice 23).** Rulings OD-205 and OD-206 in
//! `docs/decision-record.md`.
//!
//! - *Pairing (OD-205, the builder).* An OPERATOR KEY is an operation, its
//!   operand width and its signedness ([`ArithKey`]): a product's low half
//!   (`imul r, r`, `mul`'s RAX), the unsigned widening product (`mul`'s
//!   RDX:RAX), a quotient or remainder (`div`/`idiv` of the canonical
//!   dividend), a 32- or 64-bit shift by a count the walk does not know
//!   (`shl`/`shr`/`sar r, cl`, the barrel [`shift_modulo_width`] reads at
//!   log2(w) bits, the 5 or 6 bits of CL x86 reads at those widths; an 8- or
//!   16-bit one masks its count modulo 32 and is refused by name). A
//!   machine row of key K whose TV-1 stamp names exactly one source operation
//!   of key K, whose operands are no constants by value, and whose own
//!   transcription is `K(c, d)` ([`MachineArith`], [`pair_arithmetic`]) is
//!   spelled as the source's `K(a, b)`; the theorem claims `a = c` and `b =
//!   d`, each a claim of its own, unconditionally. CHECKED: the key, the
//!   transcription and both operand equations (the claims); a row of another
//!   key keeps its exact term. The pairing admits nothing ([`owner_theorems`]:
//!   the theorem without it is built first and its refusal is the body's),
//!   and is kept only where the theorem's own spelling does not absorb it;
//!   the theorem without it decides a paired theorem's refutation. RE-DERIVED
//!   by every consumer: a paired theorem carries its pairing record
//!   ([`X86BodyPairing`]: each paired row, each paired name's WALK
//!   definition, and the theorem before its spelling with the spelling's
//!   choices), from which the verifier, before it replays the theorem, and
//!   every export, before it is published, re-check that each paired name's
//!   walk definition is its part of `K(c, d)` (check (3)), that it is defined
//!   as the same part of the same key's `K(a, b)`, that the operand claims of
//!   exactly the bits `K` reads ([`ArithKey::read_operand`]) are
//!   unconditional conjuncts, and that the statement is the recorded
//!   theorem's spelling ([`spell_statement`]); a theorem whose record does
//!   not re-derive, or that has none, is never replayed or published.
//!   TRUSTED in addition: nothing (congruence of one SMT-LIB function over
//!   meanings the theorem already states; the stamp and the source key, rule
//!   (1), only choose which rows pair).
//! - *The generalization (OD-206, the replay route).* An exact replay
//!   (whole, part or claim) that is neither Valid nor refuted is followed by
//!   one replay of the obligation with every product of two non-constant
//!   terms, quotient or remainder by a non-constant divisor and variable
//!   shift a fresh free input of its width (`split::generalize`). A Valid
//!   replay credits it (instantiation); any other outcome leaves the exact
//!   one (a satisfying model is no counterexample, OD-200's rule); every
//!   consumer re-derives the generalization ([`X86BodyClaims::check`]).
//!   TRUSTED in addition: nothing.
//!
//! **Private accesses at a non-constant offset (slice 17).** Rulings OD-183
//! and OD-184 in `docs/decision-record.md`; the semantics are
//! [`indexed`]'s, stated once for both walks.
//!
//! - *Indexed addresses.* An `Iadd` of a private frame address `(slot, c)`
//!   and an `I64` of the chain that is no constant is an INDEXED address
//!   ([`IndexedAddr`]; the machine's [`RegValue::Indexed`]), with the 64-bit
//!   offset term `c + d`; it stays indexed through copies, constant offsets
//!   and field offsets, and is only ever a scalar load's or store's address
//!   or a direct call's argument (stored as data, returned, compared, joined
//!   differently, carried by a loop header, passed to a C memory routine or
//!   offset by a second non-constant: outside, by name). On the machine the
//!   offset is whatever its own rows compute (`add` of a frame register and
//!   a value, `lea` with a scaled index over one), a term, never matched
//!   against the source's.
//! - *Semantics.* An access of `w` bytes into a slot of `n` bytes has the
//!   candidate offsets `k` in `0..=n - w` congruent to `c` modulo the
//!   alignment modulus (the power of two the source's constant stride is a
//!   multiple of). A load reads the low-bit select over the candidates of
//!   what a load at each constant offset reads (a balanced `ite` tree on the
//!   bits of the offset above the modulus); a store updates every covered
//!   byte by an `ite` chain over the candidates covering it, a new private
//!   byte ([`SlotByte::Indexed`], defined where the store runs, `ix{id}`)
//!   joined like slice 9's merged bytes. An indexed call argument is
//!   observed as `slot{s} + o` on both sides (slice 13's slot address
//!   input), its slot escapes, and the machine must pass an address of the
//!   same slot ([`X86BodyMismatch::CallStackAddress`]).
//! - *Claim.* OD-84's, and: on every run on which the source is defined,
//!   every indexed access (and argument, `w = 0`) the source performs, and
//!   every one the machine performs before the source stops, lies inside its
//!   slot at a candidate offset ([`indexed::in_bounds`], each side its own
//!   offset). In-bounds is a CLAIM the solver proves, never a premise:
//!   trust-ir resolves `IntToPtr` provenance by address, and LIR lowers a GEP
//!   and integer pointer arithmetic alike, so a premise could waive a defined
//!   run. By induction over the run, the first access off its candidates is
//!   reached with every earlier term exact, where its claim is false; so a
//!   Valid theorem makes every select and update exact. Admission needs a
//!   bound the access's own region holds (a dominating guard or the index's
//!   own range, [`indexed::check_bounds`]); it only decides admission.
//! - *Spelling.* A theorem with an in-bounds claim is value-numbered and
//!   inlined as slice 16 spells a part, as one part, after every definition
//!   that only moves bits of variables is substituted (host qualification:
//!   a constant stride behind a definition name is otherwise multiplied out
//!   as a product of two variables, and a stored value the two sides name
//!   differently keeps its byte claims).
//! - *Dead bytes at a cut point* (OD-184). A private byte no path from a
//!   loop header (slice 12) or a slice-16 cut point reads before a store
//!   overwrites it ([`split::live_bytes`], one liveness for both) is left
//!   out of the cut's relation and assumptions on both sides and starts
//!   UNREADABLE ([`SlotByte::Dead`], [`MachineByte::dead`]): a read of it is
//!   no claim. A liveness error only refuses.
//!
//! **Definedness and dispatch (slice 14).** Four more parts (rulings in
//! `docs/decision-record.md`, slice 14):
//!
//! - *Division* (`Udiv`, `Urem`, `Sdiv`, `Srem` of an `I8`/`I16`/`I32`/`I64`):
//!   trust-ir's `eval_int_binop` (pin cf33f748) gives the unsigned or the
//!   truncating signed quotient and remainder (the remainder with the
//!   dividend's sign) and makes a zero divisor, and signed the minimum by -1,
//!   undefined behaviour ([`division`], [`division_undefined`]; a unit test
//!   checks both against the pinned interpreter at every width). The theorem
//!   takes the undefined case as a PER-PATH DOMAIN PREMISE, the generalization
//!   OD-57 anticipated: each point of the run (every event, every block end)
//!   has a source STOP term ([`UnitStops`]), 1 once an executed division on
//!   the way there was undefined, and every claim at that point is stated
//!   under the condition that it is 0 ([`running`], [`under`]). The stop term is path-sensitive: it starts
//!   at 0 at the entry and at a loop header, accumulates along a block, and
//!   at a DAG join is the join of the incoming edges' end terms by their
//!   guards, so a division on an untaken path adds nothing. Claims before an
//!   undefined division keep their force (stronger than trust-ir's
//!   `behaviour_refines`, under which an undefined run constrains nothing).
//!   On the machine, `div`/`idiv` ([`machine_division`], Intel SDM DIV/IDIV:
//!   the double-width dividend in EDX:EAX or RDX:RAX, a truncating quotient
//!   to EAX/RAX and the remainder to EDX/RDX, every status flag undefined)
//!   raises #DE when the divisor is zero or the quotient does not fit; the
//!   walk records the fault in the machine's STOP bit
//!   ([`MachineState::stopped`]) and goes on. Wherever the source has not
//!   stopped, the machine must not have either ([`stop_claims`]), before each
//!   event and at each block's end: a #DE (or a trap carrier) on an input
//!   where the source is defined is refuted. The dividend is exact for every
//!   high half: stated at double width in general, and at the operation's
//!   width when the high half is the selector's `xor edx, edx` (unsigned) or
//!   `cqo`/`cdq` of the low half (signed), recognized by the definitions of
//!   the names they hold. `xor r, r, r` of one register is zero and reads no
//!   value. Three spellings keep a correct lowering's theorem within a
//!   solver's reach, all exact: a signed division states its divisor's unit
//!   cases first (by 1 the dividend, by -1 its negation; [`division`]); an
//!   unsigned division by a constant power of two is its shift or mask
//!   ([`power_of_two_division`]), and the selector's magic-number `mul` by
//!   one the shifts it is ([`power_of_two_product`]); and a canonical
//!   machine division's value is restated as the source division's where
//!   their operands are equal ([`pair_divisions`]). A several-path source
//!   with a division is stated over its DAG.
//! - *The divide-by-zero guard* (`GuardDivZero { obligation: None }`, which
//!   the adapter emits before a trust-ir division annotated `DivNonZero`): a
//!   DEFINED TRAP exactly when its divisor is zero, an observable outcome,
//!   never a premise. The selector lowers it to the `TrapDivZeroExact`
//!   carrier of the divisor's register, which the pipeline expands after this
//!   theorem's subject to `test d, d; je -> ud2` at the register's width;
//!   the walk states that firing condition and records a carrier that fires
//!   before any other stop in the machine's TRAP bit. Wherever a guard fires
//!   and the source had not stopped before it, the machine's first stop
//!   before the next event is a carrier ([`stop_claims`]): a dropped guard,
//!   a guard on another register, a guard moved past an event or a #DE in
//!   its place is refuted. A guard bound to a discharged obligation (the
//!   certified-elimination kernel may delete its carrier) stays outside.
//! - *A `Switch` several of whose cases (or a case and its default) reach
//!   one block*: one source edge ([`switch_targets`]) whose test is the
//!   disjunction of theirs ([`BranchTest::Cases`]); the adapter gives a case
//!   with block arguments a copy block of its own, so such cases pass no
//!   argument and the edge loses nothing. On the machine the block is reached
//!   by several ROUTES of the compare chain (a `je` per case, the default's
//!   `jmp`; [`block_routes`]), each a real transfer; the edge's machine
//!   literal is the disjunction of the routes' `jcc` conjunctions, and the
//!   state at the block their join by those conditions ([`walk_routes`]).
//!   Such a source is stated over its DAG.
//! - *Jump tables* (the selector's dense-switch dispatch,
//!   `emit_x86_jump_table`): a bound check `cmp n, range; ja default; jmp
//!   d`, then the dispatch block `d` ([`dispatch_blocks`]): `lea b, [rip +
//!   table]`, `mov e, [b + n*8]`, `add e, b`, `jmp e`. The dispatch block
//!   lowers no LIR block of its own; it is part of the block whose final
//!   `jmp` reaches it, and its route to a target holds exactly when the
//!   index is one of the table's indices of that target ([`TableValue`]: the
//!   walk tracks the table address, entry and target, never their bits),
//!   read from the function's own table (`X86JumpTableData`). A table entry,
//!   a bound or a subtracted minimum that sends a case elsewhere is refuted.
//!   The machine-function refusal of slices 1-13 is split by cause: EH
//!   tables, guard obligations, an outgoing call area, a constant-pool entry
//!   other than slice 15's float loads, and a jump table outside the
//!   dispatch idiom each stay outside by name.
//!
//! **The value, call and observable residue (slice 15).** Five more parts:
//!
//! - *Float constants and sign-bit operations* (ruling OD-112 in
//!   `docs/decision-record.md`, which extends OD-73's bit-pattern model). LIR
//!   `Fconst { ty, imm }` is trust-ir's `float_bits_from_f64`: `imm.to_bits()`
//!   at `F64`, `(imm as f32).to_bits()` at `F32` ([`float_constant_bits`];
//!   an `F32` NaN, whose narrowed payload Rust leaves unspecified, stays
//!   outside by name). `Fneg` and `Fabs` are trust-ir's `-x` and `x.abs()`,
//!   IEEE 754 §5.5.1's sign-bit operations: the sign bit flipped or cleared,
//!   every other bit kept, NaNs included ([`FloatSignOp`]). The selector loads
//!   a constant, or the sign mask, from the function's constant pool with
//!   `movss`/`movsd xmm, [rip + entry]` (the entry's 4 or 8 bytes, the rest of
//!   the register zeroed), and flips or clears the sign with the three-operand
//!   `pxor`/`andps`/`andpd d, s, m` (bitwise over the whole register, of which
//!   the walk models the low 64 bits). A constant-pool entry is admitted only
//!   as the operand of such a load of exactly its size. A test checks the
//!   verify-owned meanings against the pinned reference interpreter on every
//!   class of pattern. Every other floating-point operation (arithmetic,
//!   compares, conversions, rounding) stays outside by name (OD-73).
//! - *Registers of mixed width.* A `MovRR` or a three-address (or tied)
//!   `add`/`sub`/`imul`/`and`/`or`/`xor` whose registers are not all of one
//!   width (the selector's assembly of a 3-byte aggregate eightbyte: `mov r64,
//!   r32` at a parameter, `mov edx, r64; or edx, r64` at an argument) defines
//!   the low 32 bits of the operation of the operands' low 32 bits, and the
//!   bits above as a free input `u_k`: the encoder emits the row with REX.W
//!   unless every register is 32-bit, so those bits are whatever the
//!   allocated registers held there, which a 32-bit register does not keep.
//!   That holds of the 32- and the 64-bit encoding alike. A private address
//!   read by such a row escapes (no claim).
//! - *A `bool` parameter forwarded* (ruling OD-113). A function that takes a
//!   `B1` parameter may pass a `B1` argument. The argument is observed as the
//!   clean 32-bit bool every callee the backend meets reads, in every caller
//!   view walked, so the psABI caller's register (bits 1..7 clear, bits 8..63
//!   anything) must be normalized before it is forwarded: the selector now
//!   defines a System V `B1` register parameter as `movzx r32, r/m8` of its
//!   argument register's low byte (before slice 15, a 32-bit copy, which this
//!   theorem refutes under the psABI view).
//! - *Accesses through a derived address* (ruling OD-114; slice 7's retention
//!   rule refused them). A load or store through an address that may derive
//!   from an escaped slot may read or write those slots' bytes as well as
//!   incoming memory. It is an event of the world trace like any incoming
//!   access (its address, a store's value, a load's one shared input `l_j`),
//!   and it also observes the escaped slots' contents at the access, like a
//!   call ([`slot_chunks`]); a store then leaves every byte of every escaped
//!   slot as the free input `t{site}s{slot}o{offset}` ([`access_byte_name`])
//!   on both sides. By induction over the trace both sides reach the access
//!   with equal incoming memory and equal escaped bytes, which together are
//!   all the memory such an address can reach (a slot that has not escaped
//!   has an address nothing outside the function holds), so a load reads one
//!   value on both sides and a store leaves one state: sharing the inputs is
//!   the congruence, as for a call. The machine must let exactly the source's
//!   slots escape by the access, else no claim.
//! - *An `I128` in the stack argument areas.* An `I128` parameter or argument
//!   without two INTEGER registers left takes two eightbytes of the incoming
//!   or outgoing argument area, the first at a displacement that is a
//!   multiple of 16, low half first (psABI §3.2.3), and a later scalar still
//!   takes a register left over ([`parameter_locations`],
//!   [`stack_arguments`]; a unit test sweeps both against the selector).
//!   The parameter is `e_stack{d + 8} ++ e_stack{d}`, and the argument is
//!   observed as its two 64-bit halves, as in registers.
//!
//! A body with no result, no call and no incoming-memory access still has
//! no observable and builds no theorem (ruling OD-115): every effect such a
//! body has on its caller (the callee-saved registers and RSP it preserves,
//! the memory it does not write, the return it reaches) is checked by the
//! trusted walk before any theorem exists, so an observable stating them
//! would be an identity, and a replayed proof of an identity proves nothing
//! about the rows it would credit.
//!
//! **Memory and values (slice 13).** Four more parts of the memory and value
//! domain:
//!
//! - *A private address stored as data.* An 8-byte `Store` whose value is a
//!   private stack address (a `StackAddr`, copied or offset as for an
//!   access) stores that address: its value is [`frame_address_term`], one
//!   free 64-bit input per slot, `slot{x}` ([`slot_address_name`]), plus the
//!   offset, on both sides (the slot binding by index makes it the same
//!   slot). Nothing is assumed about it (no alignment, no distinctness from
//!   another slot or from incoming memory): it is only ever DATA, a stored or
//!   observed value, never an access's address, which the walks still resolve
//!   without the solver. Its slot is shared from the store on with whatever
//!   can read the memory it went to ([`escape_source`], [`escape_machine`]):
//!   stored to incoming memory or into a slot that escaped already, it
//!   escapes at the store, and slice 7's model applies from there on (every
//!   later call reads and redefines its bytes, and an incoming access
//!   through an address that may derive from it observes them, slice 15's
//!   form of the retention rule); stored into a slot that has not escaped, it stays
//!   private until that slot escapes, which takes along every slot whose
//!   address it holds, transitively. Read back whole by an 8-byte load, the
//!   bytes are that address again (a tracked private address, so an access
//!   through it is private). An aggregate argument's eightbyte that is
//!   exactly one stored address is passed as that address: the walk checks
//!   the register holds exactly it, as for a private-address argument, and
//!   its slot escapes to the call. Any other read of such bytes (a part,
//!   another width, part of an eightbyte), an aggregate result holding one,
//!   and a DAG join of paths that leave different bytes where one holds an
//!   address are outside the domain. At every call the machine must let
//!   exactly the source's slots escape (else no single trace states both, and
//!   the body makes no claim). The census's 39 `core` functions of this class
//!   store a slice's or a closure capture's address into a local and pass that
//!   local on, or pass it by value.
//! - *Arguments on the stack.* An `I8`/`I16`/`I32`/`I64` parameter past the
//!   six INTEGER argument registers is the low bits of its eightbyte of the
//!   incoming argument area, the free input `e_stack{16 + 8k}` for the
//!   `k`-th ([`parameter_locations`]), which the pre-RA stream reads as
//!   `[rbp + 16 + 8k]` (the frame the post-allocation prologue builds, `push
//!   rbp; mov rbp, rsp`, puts the caller's argument area there); any other
//!   access through RBP makes no claim. An `I8`/`I16`/`I32`/`I64` argument or
//!   a private address past the argument registers ([`stack_arguments`]) is
//!   observed like a register argument, at its return bits, as the bytes the
//!   outgoing argument area holds at its eightbyte when the call runs: the
//!   selector opens the area with `sub rsp, n`, stores each argument to
//!   `[rsp + 8k]` and closes it with `add rsp, n` after the call (an
//!   unwritten byte is [`X86BodyMismatch::UndefinedRead`]; a private address
//!   must be exactly the paired one, else
//!   [`X86BodyMismatch::CallStackAddress`], and its slot escapes; any other
//!   adjustment or access of RSP makes no claim). A `bool`, float or
//!   aggregate on the stack stays outside (slice 15 admits an `I128` there).
//! - *The C memory routines.* LIR `Memcpy`, `Memmove` and `Memset` are calls
//!   to `memcpy`, `memmove` and `memset` by symbol (the selector lowers each
//!   to exactly that call) under the call model of slices 3 and 7: an
//!   uninterpreted callee of the world and its arguments on both sides,
//!   observed at the routine's C signature `(I64, I64, I64)` or `(I64, I32,
//!   I64)`, so nothing about what it copies or fills is assumed; a private
//!   address operand is a pointer and its slot escapes to the routine. That
//!   the routine the call binds to has the intrinsic's effect is trusted
//!   (the routine contract), so (ruling OD-217, OD-210's guard extended) a
//!   theorem whose module or link unit defines `memcpy`, `memmove` or
//!   `memset`, or which is that routine (its own copy would call itself),
//!   is refused ([`check_trusted_routines`]).
//! - *Atomics* ([`atomic_access`], ruling OD-83 in
//!   `docs/decision-record.md`). An `AtomicLoad` of any ordering a load may
//!   have and a `Relaxed` or `Release` `AtomicStore` of a
//!   `B1`/`I8`/`I16`/`I32`/`I64` through a non-private address are the plain
//!   access of the same bytes, trust-ir's single-threaded meaning, paired
//!   only with one machine access of that width that carries the selector's
//!   atomic proof origin (and a plain source access never with such a row).
//!   The theorem's claim is single-threaded equivalence only. A sequentially
//!   consistent store stays outside by name (the selector lowers it to a
//!   plain `mov`, which x86-TSO may reorder with a later load; the rustc
//!   bridge emits such a store as an exchange instead), and so do
//!   read-modify-write, compare-exchange, fences, `I128` atomics and an atomic
//!   access of a private slot. The `core` functions of this class dispatch on
//!   their ordering through a jump table, which stays outside.
//!
//! **Register pairs and float bit patterns (slice 11).** Three more scalar
//! carriers, each with its System V register class (psABI §3.2.3):
//!
//! - *`I128`* is two INTEGER eightbytes: a parameter or argument takes the
//!   next two integer argument registers, low half first (none is skipped;
//!   when only one is left System V passes it on the stack, outside the
//!   lane), and a result is RAX (low half) and RDX (high half); it must be
//!   the only result. The source states every `I128` operation at 128 bits
//!   with trust-ir's meaning: constants (LIR `Iconst { ty: I128 }`, whose high
//!   half is its `i64` immediate's sign extension, and `Iconst128`), copies,
//!   same-carrier bitcasts, wrapping add, subtract and multiply, and, or,
//!   xor, not, negation, population count, shifts by an in-range constant
//!   amount (of any carrier), compares, selects, extensions to and
//!   truncations from 128 bits, and loads and stores. The machine computes
//!   them on 64-bit halves with the rows the walk already models plus `adc
//!   d, a, b` and `sbb d, a, b` (Intel SDM ADC/SBB: `a + b + CF` and
//!   `a - (b + CF)`, CF from the flags a modelled row of the same block defined,
//!   as for a `setcc`; the flags they write are not modelled) and the
//!   one-operand `shl`/`shr`/`sar d, cl` (`d = d op CL`, tied like slice 8's
//!   two-operand rows). An incoming 16-byte access is two 8-byte accesses,
//!   the low half at the address and the high half at the address plus 8,
//!   which is what a non-atomic, non-volatile little-endian 16-byte access
//!   reads or writes, with nothing between the halves; each half is paired
//!   with one machine access. A private slot's 16 bytes are modelled byte by
//!   byte as before. A call's `I128` result is RAX and RDX (`r{k}`, `q{k}`,
//!   64 bits each); an `I128` field in a 16-byte aggregate (`[u128; 1]`)
//!   fills both INTEGER eightbytes. Every name the theorem defines stays at
//!   most 64 bits wide: a 128-bit source value is defined as its two halves
//!   ([`name_source_value`]). Outside, by name: a shift of an `I128` by an
//!   amount that is no constant of the chain (the selector's register-pair
//!   lowering does not reduce it modulo 128, so the refinement OD-57 states
//!   is not what it computes), a branch on an `I128`, an `I128` beside
//!   another result, and checked arithmetic of `I128` (the adapter emits
//!   none).
//! - *`F32`/`F64`* is a bit pattern in the low 32 or 64 bits of an SSE
//!   register: a parameter or argument takes the next of XMM0-XMM7
//!   (numbered apart from the integer registers), a result XMM0 (a second
//!   float lane XMM1; an integer lane beside it stays in RAX). The theorem
//!   gives a float no floating-point meaning: it is admitted only where it
//!   is moved (copies, joins, call arguments and results, returns), stored
//!   or loaded (as its bytes), or reinterpreted by a `Bitcast` to or from
//!   the integer of its width (`f32::to_bits`, `f64::from_bits`), which keeps
//!   the bits. The machine rows are `movss`/`movsd` between XMM registers
//!   (the low 32 or 64 bits copied; register allocation treats the
//!   destination as a pure definition, so the bits of a `movss` destination
//!   above 32 are a free input), `movd`/`movq` between an XMM register and a
//!   general-purpose one, and `movss`/`movsd` loads and stores. A call may
//!   change every XMM register (all are caller-saved). Every floating-point
//!   operation (arithmetic, compares, conversions; slice 15 admits constants
//!   and `Fneg`/`Fabs`, exact bit operations) stays outside by name: its IEEE
//!   754 meaning needs QF_FP, and on the
//!   qualification host AY's strict certification rejects every QF_FP proof
//!   (OD-73 in `docs/decision-record.md`). So does a 16-bit float, a
//!   vector, and an aggregate with a float field (an SSE eightbyte).
//!
//! **Aggregates in general-purpose registers (slice 8).** A parameter, the
//! result, a direct call's argument or a direct call's one result may be a
//! LIR `Struct` or `Array` that System V passes in general-purpose registers:
//! 1 to 16 bytes under the verify-owned C layout ([`layout`]), every leaf a
//! `B1`, `I8`, `I16`, `I32` or `I64` field lying wholly inside one eightbyte,
//! every eightbyte holding a field. Each eightbyte is then INTEGER (psABI
//! §3.2.3) and travels in the low bytes of one register: eightbyte `k` of a
//! parameter or argument in the next argument register, of a result in RAX,
//! then RDX ([`classify_aggregate`], verify-owned; the selector's own
//! classifier is checked against it on its real output by
//! `the_verify_owned_classification_agrees_with_the_selector`, and every
//! call row's declared registers and widths against it by the walk). An
//! enum, an aggregate over 16 bytes (MEMORY class: on the stack or through a
//! hidden buffer), a floating-point field (an SSE eightbyte), a vector field,
//! an aggregate result beside another result, and parameters that need more
//! than six argument registers are refused by name. (Slice 11 admits an
//! `I128` field that fills both eightbytes, and `I128` and float scalars.)
//!
//! LIR carries an aggregate value by its address: an aggregate parameter
//! is the address of a private copy the selector makes at entry (one more
//! slot after the source's), an aggregate call result the address of a slot
//! the selector allocates at the call (after the parameters' copies, in
//! layout order), and a value the LIR types as an aggregate (a copy of a slot
//! address hinted with the callee's parameter type, the adapter's by-value
//! argument temporary) is the aggregate stored there. The model is exact and
//! byte-level:
//!
//! - *Parameters.* The copy's bytes are the caller's: byte `b` of eightbyte
//!   `k` is byte `b` of the `k`-th argument register's entry value (only the
//!   eightbyte's own bytes; the register's bits above them are the caller's
//!   garbage, which nothing may read). Field reads are then ordinary private
//!   loads (slice 4): extracts at the layout's offsets.
//! - *Results.* The returned address's bytes at the return, eightbyte by
//!   eightbyte, are observed against the low bytes of RAX and RDX: exactly
//!   the bytes a caller may read, the bits above them not at all.
//! - *Arguments.* An aggregate argument's bytes at the call, eightbyte by
//!   eightbyte, are observed against the low bytes of its argument registers
//!   (a callee reads those bytes and no more). Its slot does not escape: the
//!   callee receives bytes, not the address (a private address in its
//!   register is [`X86BodyMismatch::CallStackAddress`]).
//! - *Call results.* The callee defines each eightbyte's bytes and nothing
//!   above them: RAX (RDX) after the call is `g_c ++ r_c` (`h_c ++ q_c`)
//!   with `r_c` as wide as the eightbyte's bytes, and the result's slot holds
//!   those bytes. Once a slot escaped, they may be an address derived from it
//!   (slice 7's retention rule).
//!
//! Padding bytes inside an eightbyte are observed like the rest: on both
//! sides they are the same bytes of the same slot or register, so this is
//! stronger than a caller needs and never weaker. Two machine forms the
//! selector's eightbyte moves use are admitted: the tied two-operand `shl d,
//! imm`, `shr d, imm` and `or d, s` (`d = d op x`; register allocation keeps
//! the destination as a def and a use), and a byte or word load into, or
//! store from, a physical register's 8- or 16-bit alias (`mov al, m8`, `mov
//! [m], dx`), whose bits above the loaded ones are a free input `u_k` (the
//! narrow-load rule). The aggregate's slot binding is by index like every
//! slot's, and the machine's slot table must be the source's plus the
//! copies, at each aggregate's size and alignment (the frame is laid out
//! from it); soundness does not rest on which machine slot holds which copy,
//! because the source defines every byte of a copy and the machine every
//! byte it walks.
//!
//! **Calls through memory (slice 7).** A direct call may receive a private
//! stack slot's address (a `StackAddr`, copied or offset by a constant, as
//! for a load) as an ordinary 64-bit argument: an out-parameter (`&mut
//! local`, `&local`), or the hidden return buffer of a callee in
//! `Function::sret_arg_callees`, which is its argument 0 (the selector
//! places it in RDI, the first integer argument, as System V requires, and
//! such a call has no result). The argument is the slot's ADDRESS, the `I64`
//! pointer type: an aggregate passed by value, which LIR represents by its
//! slot's address too but types by the sidecar hint as the aggregate (the
//! selector passes its bytes, not the address), is refused by the type-hint
//! check and never taken for a pointer. The slot then ESCAPES: from that
//! call on it is shared with the callee world, and the call model of slice 3
//! extends to it exactly, in Ackermann-reduced form:
//!
//! - *Inputs.* A call's observable inputs are its scalar arguments and the
//!   contents, at the call, of every slot that escaped to it or to an
//!   earlier call on the path: a callee may read any byte of a slot whose
//!   address it receives (pointer arithmetic within the slot is defined; a
//!   `&T` argument carries no read-only promise LIR states), and it may
//!   retain an escaped address for a later callee. Each slot is observed in
//!   8-byte chunks from offset 0 (the last one shorter): a chunk that is one
//!   store's bytes is that value, any other the concatenation of its bytes
//!   ([`slot_chunks`]); a chunk both sides spell alike (bytes neither side
//!   wrote, or the bytes an earlier call left) states nothing and is not
//!   stated. A private address argument is no SMT term: the walk checks that
//!   the machine passes exactly the paired slot and offset (a tracked
//!   private address of another slot or offset, or one where the source
//!   passes a value, is [`X86BodyMismatch::CallStackAddress`]; an untracked
//!   value where the source passes one makes no claim).
//! - *Outputs.* Besides its scalar result, the call leaves every byte of
//!   every escaped slot as it chooses (it may write each byte, padding
//!   included, or leave it as it was): byte `y` of slot `x` after call site
//!   `c` is the free input `d{c}s{x}o{y}` on both sides. By induction over
//!   the calls, a Valid theorem means each call receives equal arguments and
//!   equal escaped bytes in the same world on both sides, so it leaves equal
//!   bytes and an equal result: sharing the inputs is exactly the UF
//!   congruence, without assuming the callee writes any byte or keeps any.
//!   A caller read after the call reads those inputs; a machine that reads a
//!   slot byte before the call where the source reads it after, keeps a
//!   value across the call, reads another slot or offset, or treats the
//!   result as the old contents is refuted. A `bool` read of one
//!   call-defined byte is trust-ir's `Load Bool`, `byte != 0`, which the
//!   selector computes for every bool load.
//! - *Retention.* A slot stays escaped for the rest of the path, so every
//!   later call (whatever its arguments) reads and redefines it too. Once
//!   any slot escaped, what a call returns, what an incoming load reads and
//!   what a call left in a slot may be an address derived from it, and so
//!   may anything computed from them: an incoming load or store through such
//!   an address could touch the slot behind the byte model's back, so it was
//!   outside the domain until slice 15, which observes the escaped slots'
//!   contents at such an access (see **The value, call and observable
//!   residue**). Every other
//!   incoming address (computed from parameters, symbols, constants, and
//!   loads or call results from before the first escape) existed before the
//!   slot's address left the function and stays disjoint from it, as in
//!   slice 4. The slot's address itself is still only a load/store address,
//!   a call argument or (slice 13) stored data: returning or computing with
//!   it is outside the domain.
//!
//! The callee contract extends accordingly (see **Trusted**): a callee reads
//! and writes incoming memory and the bytes of the escaped slots only, and
//! its result, next world and new slot bytes depend only on its register
//! arguments, the world and those bytes. Nothing about the callee's own
//! semantics is assumed: an LLVM callee may write every byte of a return
//! buffer or of `*out`, padding included, or leave any of them, and the
//! model covers each choice. Neither does it check that the slot is as
//! large or as aligned as the callee's pointee type: LIR states neither (the
//! slot table's size and alignment are the adapter's, and the frame layout
//! that honours them is outside this theorem, as for every slot). A slot
//! smaller than what its callee writes is an error of the LIR source, not of
//! its lowering.
//!
//! **Acyclic control flow (slice 6).** The LIR CFG may be any DAG
//! ([`source_cfg`]), each block ending in one terminator: `Jump`, `Brif` on
//! a scalar (`then` when it is not 0), `Switch` on a scalar (each case the
//! adapter's normalized constant of the selector's carrier, no two cases of
//! one value, every edge to a different block), `Return` or `Trap`. Another
//! terminator or an unreachable block is
//! [`X86BodyUnsupported::SourceShape`], naming which (a back edge is slice
//! 12's, below). A source of at most
//! [`MAX_SOURCE_BLOCKS`] blocks, [`MAX_PATHS`] entry-to-end paths and
//! [`MAX_PATH_INSTRUCTIONS`] instructions on one path is stated path by path
//! as below; a larger one over its DAG (slice 9, below). The source is its
//! paths ([`enumerate_paths`], depth first, `then` before `else`, cases
//! before the default), each admitted as a chain under the slice 1-5 rules; a join's
//! block parameters are the values the predecessor's copies (the adapter's
//! lowering of block arguments) defined on that path. A path ending in
//! `Trap` observes its trace and the trap; it returns nothing. A one-path
//! body keeps the slice 1-5 theorem byte for byte.
//!
//! *Pairing.* Each source path is paired with the machine path it must run
//! through a one-to-one correspondence of machine and LIR blocks
//! ([`block_correspondence`]), found from the rows' TV-1 lowering provenance,
//! never from block ids: at O1+ passes renumber the surviving blocks to
//! `0..n` after deleting some, so an id names no LIR block reliably, while a
//! row keeps the stamp of the LIR instruction it lowers wherever a pass moves
//! it. A machine block lowers LIR block B when every source-attributed row
//! in it is stamped with B; the correspondence needs one such machine block
//! per LIR block THAT A SOURCE PATH REACHES (the entry lowering the entry)
//! and no other laid-out block, and each such machine block's successors must
//! be, through what they lower, exactly its LIR block's successors. A LIR
//! block no path reaches is dead code a pass left behind: it carries no claim,
//! and the correspondence's own conditions prove the machine block lowering it
//! (if any survived) off the chain from the machine entry, so it cannot run. From the entry block, rows run in
//! order; a `jcc` is taken exactly when its target lowers the source path's
//! next block, and not taken otherwise; a `jmp` or a fallthrough (to the
//! next block in `block_order`) reaches the block that lowers it; the path's
//! last block ends in its `ret` (a returning path) or `ud2` (a trapping
//! path). Every step is a real machine transfer, so the paired rows are what
//! the machine executes whenever the theorem's `jcc` literals hold: the
//! pairing decides only WHICH machine path each source path is judged
//! against, and soundness never rests on it; the theorem states the
//! condition under which the machine takes each `jcc`. Under the
//! correspondence every source path has exactly one paired machine path, so
//! control can disagree only in a `jcc`'s condition (refuted by the theorem)
//! or in a path's end (a `ret` where the source traps, or a `ud2` where it
//! returns: [`X86BodyMismatch::Control`]). Without it (a block merged,
//! except a chain of single-entry `Jump`s (slice 24a), if-converted, split,
//! added, proved unreachable and deleted, or a transfer retargeted by an
//! optimizer pass) there is no claim, never a named mismatch; a `jcc` whose
//! fallthrough also reaches its target makes none either. Slice 24a (ruling
//! OD-226): where the original pair has no correspondence and its walk needs
//! one, the theorem is stated over the exact merged-chain quotient of the
//! pair ([`quotient`]): each chain of LIR blocks joined by an argument-free
//! `Jump` into a block no other edge enters, that one machine block's stamps
//! name, contracted into its head, the machine's stamps renumbered into its
//! coordinates; it binds the original pair. Slice 24c (ruling OD-228): a
//! machine block that lowers a single-entry acyclic region of LIR blocks (an
//! if-converted diamond, its stamp-silent arms, a chain the quotient
//! declines) is paired with every source path through it ([`segment`]): its
//! head is lowered one to one, its other members are covered, its successors
//! are exactly the region's exit targets, and every run through the region
//! is the machine block's one run. A one-path source whose machine has no conditional jump is the
//! slice 1-5 machine chain instead, whatever its blocks (a laid-out block off
//! the chain is [`X86BodyMismatch::UnreachableBlock`], unless it lowers a LIR
//! block no source path reaches). Each laid-out
//! block's recorded successors must be exactly the targets of its transfers,
//! as a set (the branch-layout pass at O1+ inverts a `jcc` and swaps its
//! fallthrough without reordering them).
//!
//! *Flags.* A `jcc`, like slice 5's `setcc` and a `cmovcc`, reads the flags a
//! compare (`CmpRR`/`CmpRI`: `lhs - rhs`) or a test (`TestRR`/`TestRI`:
//! `lhs & rhs`, CF and OF clear, Intel SDM TEST) OF ITS OWN BLOCK wrote,
//! through the shared EFLAGS model (`x86_64_eflags`): every condition code,
//! signed and unsigned orders, equality, sign, overflow and parity, with no
//! LIR-to-x86 condition table. Flags never cross a block boundary (a reader
//! after one makes no claim), a call leaves them undefined (a reader is
//! [`X86BodyMismatch::UndefinedRead`] of `rflags`), and every other admitted
//! ALU row writes them with semantics the walk does not model (a reader after
//! one makes no claim, so a flag writer between a compare and its jump is
//! refused, not trusted). Between selection and emission, register
//! allocation inserts only moves and spill moves (flag-neutral) and treats a
//! `cmovcc` destination as tied (it keeps its value when the condition
//! fails), which is what the walk assumes; block layout, branch relaxation
//! and the JCC-erratum padding change encodings and placement, not the
//! transfers this theorem states (TV-5/ENC-3 cover them). At O1+ the
//! optimizer's own rewrites (the fused `cmp; jcc`, a branch laid out as a
//! fallthrough, `test r, r`) are walked like the selector's rows. An
//! if-converted diamond is a segment (slice 24c, below): its machine block
//! is walked whole once per exit, and its `cmov` reads the flags the head's
//! compare wrote in that same block.
//!
//! *Theorem.* With several paths the observable is one claim per source path
//! P, in enumeration order, stated against `true`:
//!
//! ```text
//! (= true (and (or (not C_P1) (and M_P1 O_P1)) (and ... (or (not C_Pn) (and M_Pn O_Pn)))))
//! ```
//!
//! C_P conjoins the source's branch literals on P (`v != 0` or `v == 0` of a
//! `Brif`, `v == k` of a `Switch` case, `v != k` for every case of its
//! default), M_P the machine's `jcc` literals on the paired path (the x86
//! condition where the path jumps, its negation where it does not; the psABI
//! walk, then the clean-bool walk), O_P the path's observed items (the slice
//! 1-5 observable of that path). The source paths' conditions partition the
//! inputs, so the claims state that whenever the source runs P the machine
//! runs the paired path with equal observables: a machine that branches on
//! another condition, swaps its successors, traps where the source returns
//! (or the reverse) or takes another arm's value is refuted. The
//! Ackermann-reduced calls and incoming accesses stay sound path by path (by
//! induction along the path, as in slices 3 and 4): a call's result, an
//! incoming load's bytes and a frame byte's initial value are one free input
//! per SITE, which every path through the site shares and no path reads
//! twice.
//!
//! *Why this encoding.* The spelling is chosen by host qualification (AY
//! 0.27.0 build 10530, Clean 5f09e36c), per shape, never per function. The
//! implication is spelled `(or (not C) X)`: spelled `(=> C X)`, AY prints its
//! proof with a `hole` step (`ay.proof.decline
//! mechanism=printer-rule-without-external-spelling`) that Clean rejects as
//! holey, on every fixture (AY builds 12713 and 13556, the qualification
//! AYs since 2026-10-05 and 2026-10-19, keep a trusted step in it and
//! publish no proof). A several-path theorem defines no name for a
//! copy (a value that is only another name) and states only the definitions
//! its claims depend on (their cone of influence), both satisfiability-
//! preserving: on the Darwin `core` census's 345 several-path theorems, AY
//! and Clean certify 162 with copies defined and 281 without, and none that
//! certifies with them fails without (with every definition stated, 104 of
//! the first 244; with the cone alone, 125). The merged `ite` of the
//! observable (merging at the joins: the path taken, and each item, chosen
//! by nested `ite`s over the conditions) and the nested `ite` case split
//! certify on the fixtures but state the same fact less directly; the case
//! split certifies 4 of 119 core theorems the canonical claims do not. The
//! qualification test replays each rejected spelling of every fixture.
//!
//! *Selects (slice 6).* LIR `Select { cond: NotEqual }` is `then` when its
//! test is not 0, else `other`; the selector lowers it `mov d, other; cmp t,
//! 0; cmovne d, then`, a `cmovcc` (64-bit) or `cmovcc32` (32-bit, which
//! zero-extends in both cases) whose destination keeps its value when the
//! condition fails. Other select conditions are outside the domain.
//!
//! *Partially written private slots (slice 6).* A path may read a private
//! stack byte no store on it wrote (the payload of an `Option::None` built
//! on one path of a join): the byte is the slot's initial value, the free
//! input `f_k` that the machine reads at that frame byte too. That is
//! stronger than the source needs: trust-ir reads such a byte as poison,
//! which any value refines, so a machine that returns the frame's byte is
//! correct and one that computes the value some other way (a zeroed slot,
//! also a valid refinement) is refuted rather than credited. A `bool` is
//! still read back only from a byte a `bool` store wrote.
//!
//! **The DAG (slice 9).** A several-path source past the slice-6 bounds
//! (more than [`MAX_SOURCE_BLOCKS`] blocks, more than [`MAX_PATHS`] paths or
//! a path of more than [`MAX_PATH_INSTRUCTIONS`] instructions;
//! [`SourceCfg::per_path`]) is stated over its DAG, in size linear in its
//! blocks, with no bound on its paths. Any admitted source has at most
//! [`MAX_BODY_BLOCKS`] blocks and [`MAX_BODY_INSTRUCTIONS`] instructions
//! (the machine at most [`MAX_MACHINE_BLOCKS`] blocks and
//! [`MAX_MACHINE_ROWS`] rows); a one-path source keeps the slice 1-5 chain
//! whatever its length. A source with a loop is slice 12's (below).
//!
//! *Admission* ([`admitted_dag`]). Each block is admitted once, in a
//! topological order, under the slice 1-6 rules, from the state the paths
//! into it share: its one predecessor's end state, or at a join the merge of
//! its predecessors' ([`merge_admitted`]): a value every incoming edge
//! defines at one carrier (and one private address, if it is one) is kept,
//! its constant, affine value and aggregate type only where the edges agree;
//! a private byte the edges leave differently is a merged byte
//! ([`SlotByte::Merged`], an unwritten one the slot's initial byte `f_k`).
//! A `Switch` of two cases of one value and a block that redefines a value
//! some path into it defined are outside the domain. (A join of paths on
//! which different private slots escaped was too; slice 12 makes each slot
//! that escaped on some incoming path escaped at the join, below.) Each check a chain makes is made
//! on every path through the block, so the DAG admits a body only when every
//! path of it would be admitted, up to the precision a merge loses.
//!
//! *Machine walk* ([`walk_dag`]). Each machine block, the one that lowers
//! the LIR block under the slice-6 correspondence (required), starts from
//! its entry state: the machine's entry, its one predecessor's state on the
//! edge into it, or at a join the merge of the incoming edges' states
//! ([`merge_machine`]), whose registers and private bytes the edges leave
//! differently are joined by the SOURCE edges' guards (below); flags never
//! cross the boundary. A block that ends the function is walked to its
//! `ret` or `ud2`; any other is walked once per outgoing source edge over
//! the rows that run on the way to the block that lowers the edge's target
//! ([`block_rows`]: the `jcc` that reaches it taken, every earlier one not),
//! each walk against the block's own fragment, so every edge runs exactly
//! the block's calls and accesses. A root the edges leave holding different
//! private addresses, or an address and bits, is [`RegValue::Mixed`] (a read
//! makes no claim).
//!
//! *Theorem* ([`build_theorem`]). Guards are 1-bit definitions: the entry
//! block's guard is 1; a block with one incoming edge has that edge's guard;
//! a join `J` has `c{J} = (bvor G_e1 ... G_ek)` over its incoming edges; an
//! edge `e` of a conditional block `B` to `S` has `c{B}t{S} = (bvand g_B
//! (ite T_e #b1 #b0))` (just the `ite` when `g_B` is 1), `T_e` the
//! conjunction of the edge's slice-6 branch literals, and an edge of a
//! `Jump` has `g_B`. At a join, each kept value whose incoming terms differ
//! is `(ite (= G_e1 #b1) v_1 (ite ... v_k))`, a merged byte `y{id}` the same
//! choice of the bytes the edges bring, and each machine register and
//! private byte the edges leave differently the same choice over the same
//! source guards. In topological order, each block contributes its observed
//! items O_B (the slice 1-8 items of its calls, accesses and return), stated
//! as `(or (not (= g_B #b1)) O_B)` (the entry block's unguarded), and each
//! edge `e` of a conditional block the machine's `jcc` literals M_e on the
//! rows that take it (psABI walk, then clean-bool walk), stated as `(or (not
//! (= G_e #b1)) M_e)`; all in one right-nested conjunction against `true`:
//!
//! ```text
//! (= true (and K_1 (and K_2 ... K_n)))
//! ```
//!
//! *Claim.* The theorem is EQUIVALENT to the slice-6 per-path conjunction
//! `(and (or (not C_P) (and M_P O_P)) ...)` over every entry-to-end path P,
//! input by input, never weaker. For every assignment of the free inputs,
//! the source's edge tests select exactly one path P (a `Brif`'s two tests
//! and a `Switch`'s distinct cases and default each partition the tested
//! value). By induction over the topological order, `g_B = 1` exactly for
//! the blocks of P and `G_e = 1` exactly for its edges, so at each join on P
//! exactly one incoming guard holds and every joined source value equals
//! its value along P, and every joined machine value its value along the
//! machine path paired with P (the walks run the same rows from the same
//! states). The conjunction then states exactly the claims of P's blocks and
//! edges, which are M_P and O_P, and nothing else (every other claim's guard
//! is 0), while C_P holds and every other path's C_Q fails: both sides are
//! M_P ∧ O_P. The slice-9 equivalence test checks this on every slice-6
//! fixture, refutation and variant and every slice-9 fixture by sampling
//! the two theorems with their inputs matched by what each stands for. A
//! machine that branches on another condition, swaps or retargets a
//! successor, takes another arm's value or misses an edge is refuted, or
//! breaks the correspondence (no claim), exactly as in slice 6.
//!
//! *Why this spelling (slice 9).* Chosen by host qualification (AY 0.27.0
//! build 10530, Clean 5f09e36c), per shape. A DAG's theorem, and a one-path
//! chain past [`MAX_PATH_INSTRUCTIONS`] instructions
//! ([`PathEncoding::trim_long_chains`]), defines no name for a copy, states
//! only the definitions its claims reach, and substitutes every definition
//! it uses at most once into that use ([`inline_single_use`]), dropping each
//! claim equation the substitution makes an identity, true on every input
//! ([`drop_identities`], to a fixpoint); all are satisfiability-preserving,
//! and an inlined theorem that fails the structural check falls back to the
//! uninlined spelling. On that host AY's strict certification
//! rejects its own proof of a theorem with many definitions within 0.1 s (a
//! `trust` or `hole` step), even a certified one padded with twenty unused
//! ones; of the 52 Darwin `core` functions slice 9 newly binds offline, 1
//! certifies with every copy and definition stated, 6 trimmed and 22 trimmed
//! and inlined. The 1-bit `bvand`/`bvor` guards and Boolean-connective guards
//! (`PathEncoding::bool_guards`) certify the same fixtures; the slice-6
//! per-path claims of the same pairs certify fewer (the slice-9
//! qualification test replays each spelling of every fixture). On the 25
//! slice-6 fixtures the DAG theorem and the per-path one certify alike; those
//! keep the per-path spelling, byte for byte.
//!
//! **Loops (slice 12).** A source with back edges is admitted when its CFG
//! is reducible: every back edge's target (a loop HEADER) dominates its
//! source (a latch), so every cycle passes a header, and no header is the
//! entry block ([`natural_loops`]; an irreducible cycle is
//! [`X86BodyUnsupported::SourceShape`]). The theorem is translation
//! validation by simulation over the CUT POINTS: the entry block and every
//! loop header.
//!
//! *Regions* ([`source_regions`]). The region of a cut point is every block
//! a run reaches from it before it reaches a cut point again: a DAG (a
//! cycle would pass a header), stated with slice 9's machinery from the cut
//! point's assumption, its guards relative to the region's run (`c{H}x{B}`,
//! `c{H}x{B}t{S}`). An edge into a cut point is a CUT EDGE: the region's run
//! ends there. A header's parameters (the loop-carried values) are defined
//! by the copies the adapter places before the `Jump` into it ([`CutEdge`]:
//! run in order, a cycle through a temporary); they are the edge's, not
//! the block's.
//!
//! *The cut-point relation* ([`LoopHeader`], [`cut_edge_relation`]). At a
//! header `H` the source and the machine share one free input per
//! parameter, `x{H}v{i}`, and one per private byte the loop may change,
//! `x{H}b{slot}o{offset}` (a header byte: some edge into `H` leaves it other
//! than the entry edge does). The parameter's machine register is the one
//! the machine's copy on the header's one entry edge writes (its TV-1
//! stamp names the LIR copy; [`loop_pairing`]); at the header its low bits
//! are the parameter zero-extended (a `bool` the clean 32-bit carrier under
//! the clean-bool walk, its byte under the psABI walk, where a `bool`
//! argument's register holds the caller's bits 8 and up), the bits above a
//! free input `x{H}t{i}`/`x{H}c{i}` ([`ParamRoot`]). On EVERY cut edge into
//! `H`, under the edge's guard, the theorem states the relation: each
//! parameter's value on the edge equals the low bits of its register at the
//! end of the edge's walk, and each header byte's source byte equals the
//! machine's. Everything else the region assumes is what the entry edge
//! brought, exactly, and is checked to be unchanged on every edge into the
//! header: a source value is defined once (only a header's parameters are
//! defined again, by the edge copies), a private byte some edge changes is
//! a header byte, and a machine register some edge changes and no
//! parameter pairs is [`RegValue::Carried`], whose read makes no claim (the
//! machine's loop-carried registers must be exactly the header's
//! parameters); a private byte the machine's loop changes and the source's
//! does not makes no claim. The assumptions are a fixpoint over every edge
//! into each header ([`admitted_loops`], [`walk_loops`]): each round only
//! grows them, at most [`MAX_LOOP_ROUNDS`]. A slot that escaped on some
//! edge into a header is escaped at the header, and at a DAG join (also
//! slice 9's) a slot that escaped on some incoming path is escaped after
//! it: on a path on which it had not, every later call observes its bytes
//! (an item both sides must state equal) and redefines them as that call's
//! free inputs, the same on both sides; the run's real bytes, which a
//! callee that could not reach them leaves unchanged and which are equal on
//! both sides by that item, are one value of those inputs.
//!
//! *Claim.* For every run of the source, terminating or not, the machine
//! runs the paired path of every region instance, with an equal world
//! trace (calls, incoming accesses), and ends the same way: equal return
//! bits, or a trap; a source run that never ends is matched by a machine
//! run that never ends, with the same trace. Soundness, by induction over
//! the region instances of a run: at the start of an instance of `H`'s
//! region the two states satisfy the relation (the cut edge that entered
//! stated it), every value and register the region assumes from the entry
//! edge holds what it held when the loop was entered (nothing changed it,
//! checked above; a nested loop's entry is inside one iteration of the
//! enclosing loop), and the world is equal (the traces so far are). So the
//! actual run is an instance of the theorem's free inputs (the header's
//! inputs the actual parameters and bytes, the entering instance's inputs
//! its actual values, each call and load site's input its result in this
//! instance: a region is a DAG, so a site runs at most once in it), the
//! region's claims hold of it, and the instance ends at an exit with equal
//! observables or at a cut edge with the relation. A region's claims are a
//! conjunct of their own, universally quantified over the inputs they
//! share with other regions' (`forall` distributes over `and`). trust-ir
//! defines a loop that never ends (divergence is an outcome of its
//! `behaviour_refines`): the theorem claims no less for it (OD-84 in
//! `docs/decision-record.md`).
//!
//! **Scalar pairs (slice 5).** A function with two results returns them in
//! the System V integer return registers, lane 0 in RAX and lane 1 in RDX
//! (psABI §3.2.3; a Rust `ScalarPair` return, which the bridge declares as
//! two LIR results, lands there): the observable carries each lane at its
//! own return bits, in lane order. A third result leaves the C return file
//! for TrustCG's internal multi-result convention and is outside the domain.
//! A pair PARAMETER is two LIR parameters, numbered like any other: the two
//! lanes take the next two System V argument registers (the bridge declares
//! each `PassMode::Pair` argument as two independent values, rustc's own
//! convention); a lane past the sixth register is on the stack, which is
//! outside the domain. A call with two results defines RAX and RDX at the
//! bits each lane's type defines (`r_c`/`q_c`, with `g_c`/`h_c` above a
//! narrow lane), and the call row must declare exactly those result
//! registers in that order ([`X86BodyMismatch::CallMetadata`] otherwise).
//! A `bool` lane of such a call defines bit 0 alone, where the only `bool`
//! result of a call defines AL: rustc returns a `ScalarPair` as an LLVM
//! struct whose `i1` carries no `zeroext`, so LLVM leaves bits 1..7 of the
//! lane's byte unspecified (`Carrier::call_result_bits`), and a caller that
//! reads the byte without masking it is refuted.
//! RDX is caller-saved: after a call with fewer than two results it holds
//! nothing, and reading it is [`X86BodyMismatch::UndefinedRead`]. An RDX that
//! holds no defined value at the RET of a pair is
//! [`X86BodyMismatch::UndefinedReturn`].
//!
//! **Symbols (slice 5).** LIR `GlobalRef { name }` (the direct, PC-relative
//! address of an in-module symbol) and `ExternRef { name }` (the address the
//! symbol's GOT slot holds, for a symbol that may be imported or preempted)
//! are each one free 64-bit input `sym_j` per symbol and form, in the
//! source's first-reference order, shared with the machine row that takes
//! the same address: `lea r, [rip + name]` (`LeaRip`) for the direct form and
//! `mov r, [rip + name@GOT]` (`MovRipRel`) for the GOT form. The theorem
//! assumes nothing about the address (no alignment, no non-null, no
//! distinctness between symbols): loads and stores through it are
//! incoming-memory accesses under the slice-4 trace rules. A symbol-address
//! row that names a symbol, or a form, the source never references is
//! [`X86BodyMismatch::Symbol`]: under interposition the two forms can
//! differ, so a GOT load is never a substitute for a direct address or the
//! reverse. A swap of two referenced symbols is judged by the theorem.
//!
//! **Branch-free compares (slice 5).** LIR `Icmp { cond }` of two operands of
//! one carrier is the `bool` 1 exactly when `cond` holds of them at that
//! width (exact signed and unsigned orders; equality). The machine side is
//! `cmp lhs, rhs` (`CmpRR` of one width, or `CmpRI` with a sign-extended
//! `imm32`), whose flags are the shared x86 EFLAGS model of `lhs - rhs`
//! (`x86_64_eflags::encode_cmp_eflags`: CF, ZF, SF, OF and PF), then
//! `setcc r8` of an x86 condition over those flags
//! (`x86_64_eflags::eval_x86_condition`), then the selector's `movzx` of
//! that byte. The selector's own operand extensions (a narrow signed order
//! sign-extends both operands, any other narrow or bool compare
//! zero-extends and masks them) are ordinary rows the walk judges. So a
//! wrong condition, a swapped operand or signedness, or a missing extension
//! refutes the theorem, and no table maps LIR conditions to x86 ones.
//! `setcc` writes only the low byte and register allocation treats its
//! destination as a pure definition, so bits 8..63 are a free input `u_k`: a
//! `bool` result that relies on them (a `setcc` without the zero extension)
//! is refuted. RFLAGS is undefined at entry and after a call (a `setcc` of
//! it is [`X86BodyMismatch::UndefinedRead`] of `rflags`), and every ALU row
//! rewrites it with semantics the walk does not model (a `setcc` after one
//! makes no claim). The compared `bool` may be returned, stored, passed or
//! widened; a compare that feeds a branch is control flow, outside the
//! domain with every branch. A signed order of two `B1` operands (LIR orders
//! a 1-bit value as signed, so `true` is -1) is outside the domain: no Rust
//! comparison of `bool`s is signed.
//!
//! **Memory (slice 4).** Two kinds of memory, with different claims:
//!
//! *Private stack slots.* The source's `StackAddr` slots and the machine's
//! `StackSlot(s)` operands (`lea r, [slot + disp]`, or a load/store through
//! one) are bound by index: the two slot tables must be the same table (the
//! selector copies the source's), else no claim. Each slot is a distinct
//! private region of the frame, disjoint from every other slot and from all
//! incoming memory, and both trusted walks model it exactly, byte by byte
//! (fixed slots of at most [`MAX_SLOT_BYTES`] bytes each and, since slice 20,
//! [`MAX_SLOT_TOTAL_BYTES`] in all, however many there are).
//! A slot address is only ever a load/store address, copied or offset by a
//! constant, or (slice 7) a direct call's argument, which makes the slot
//! escape to that call and every later one (see **Calls through memory**),
//! or (slice 13) an 8-byte store's value (see **Memory and values**), or
//! (slice 25, OD-227) the base of a scaled-index load or store, `[slot +
//! index*scale + disp]`; any other use (a value, a return) is outside the
//! domain. A scaled-index load or store is its base-only sibling's access at
//! the address `LeaSib` computes from its operand, by `LeaSib`'s own
//! arithmetic: a private address at a constant or a variable index (slices 4
//! and 17), or incoming memory.
//! Slot contents are never observable, except an escaped slot's at a call. The source never reads a private byte it did not
//! write (outside the domain), and a `bool` is read back only from a byte a
//! `bool` store wrote; a machine read of a byte the body never wrote is a
//! free input `u_k`, so a result that depends on it is refuted. A machine
//! access outside its slot is [`X86BodyMismatch::StackSlotBounds`].
//!
//! *Incoming memory* (everything reachable through a non-private address) is
//! one byte-addressed world that every callee also reads and writes. The
//! theorem states it in Ackermann-reduced form, like the calls: the walk
//! pairs the machine's incoming loads and stores with the source's by their
//! position in the world trace of calls and accesses, with the same kind and
//! width (else [`X86BodyUnsupported::AccessTrace`]: no claim); a paired load
//! reads the one free input `l_j` on both sides; and the observable carries
//! every access's address and every store's value, in trace order. By
//! induction over the trace, a Valid theorem means the two sides reach every
//! access (and call) with equal incoming memory, so paired loads read equal
//! bytes, and they end with equal incoming memory: the final incoming memory
//! is observed through the stores that made it. No disjointness between
//! incoming pointers is assumed (any two may alias) and no access moves
//! across a call. A memory-destination `add`/`xor` (slice 25) is two accesses
//! of that trace at one address: an 8-byte load, then an 8-byte store of the
//! loaded value plus (or xor) its register; one of a private slot is outside
//! the domain. A machine body whose accesses the exact affine evaluation
//! finds may be a reordering of the source's (a permutation that moves each
//! stored value with its address and, when every address is known, keeps the
//! order of the accesses to one location) is not judged: a reordering of
//! disjoint accesses can be correct. One whose paired address or stored
//! value that evaluation shows to differ otherwise is
//! [`X86BodyMismatch::MemoryAddress`]/[`X86BodyMismatch::MemoryValue`]. A
//! same-kind reordering the evaluation cannot see is stated positionally and
//! refuted, even when it happens to be correct (the O0 selector emits
//! accesses in source order).
//! A `bool` loaded from incoming memory is the byte `l_j != 0` (8-bit
//! `l_j`, no validity premise): trust-ir reads a stored bool byte as
//! `byte != 0` (its reference interpreter's `Load`), the adapter lowers that
//! to LIR `Load B1` unchanged, and neither LIR nor trust-ir states that
//! incoming memory holds only the bytes 0 and 1 (Rust's validity invariant
//! does, but it is not a trust-ir rule). The machine side reads the same
//! byte, so a lowering that returns or passes it without computing `!= 0`
//! (the selector's `movzx; add 255; shr 8`) is refuted by any byte other
//! than 0 and 1. A private slot's `bool` is read back only from a byte a
//! `bool` store wrote, which is 0 or 1, so there the read is the stored
//! bool itself.
//!
//! *Addresses.* An address is a pointer value plus constant offsets: LIR
//! `Iadd` of a constant of the chain (an `Iconst`, or an integer operation
//! of such constants, folded exactly; the machine likewise folds 64-bit
//! `imul`/logic/`shl` of constants), or `StructGep`, whose field offset the source
//! translator computes with its own C layout ([`field_offset`], System V
//! sizes and alignments, independent of the selector's `Type::offset_of`,
//! which a unit test compares it with). The machine forms the same address
//! with `lea`, `add` of a constant register, or the operand's displacement.
//! (OD-185) LIR `ArrayGep { elem_ty }` of `(base, index)` is `base + index *
//! size(elem_ty)` at 64 bits, wrapping: the element's natural-C size under
//! the same layout ([`array_element_stride`], compared with the selector's
//! `Type::bytes` by a unit test), the 64-bit index unextended (the adapter
//! extends a narrower trust-ir index to `I64` before the opcode). The
//! selector forms it as `lea [b + i*s]` for a size of 1, 2, 4 or 8,
//! `lea [b + i*(s-1)]; add i` for 3, 5 or 9, and `imul i, s; add b`
//! otherwise. Over a private stack address it is a constant offset at a
//! constant index of the chain, and at any other `I64` index slice 17's
//! indexed address with the element size as its stride.
//!
//! *sret.* A function with a leading hidden return buffer (LIR
//! `abi_sret_param` at index 0, no return value) returns its result through
//! that buffer: its stores there are incoming stores like any other, and
//! System V requires it to return the buffer's address in RAX (psABI
//! §3.2.3), which is its observed return value (64 bits, the buffer
//! parameter). A body that returns anything else there is refuted, or named
//! ([`X86BodyMismatch::ReturnValue`]/[`X86BodyMismatch::UndefinedReturn`]).
//!
//! *Why this encoding.* It is the one canonical memory encoding, chosen by
//! host qualification, never per function: one `(Array (_ BitVec 64)
//! (_ BitVec 8))` incoming memory with `store` chains, and a byte memory
//! whose initial contents are a 1-ary uninterpreted function under `ite`
//! store chains, both state the same facts, and on the qualification host
//! (toolchain AY 0.27.0 build 10530, Clean 5f09e36c) AY rejects its own
//! proof of each (`strict UNSAT proof validation failed: step t0/t1 uses
//! unverified trust rule`) or refuses to publish it (#8821); an Ackermannized
//! byte memory certifies only without loads. The access trace is pure
//! `QF_BV`, and every memory FIXTURE shape of the test module certifies on
//! that host. (AY builds 12713 and 13556, the qualification AYs since
//! 2026-10-05 and 2026-10-19, certify five of the six rejected forms the
//! test replays; the trace still certifies every fixture shape, and the
//! encoding is never chosen per AY.) Admitted bodies beyond the fixtures do
//! not all certify (LIMITATIONS.md records the measured corpus and `core` gaps with their
//! exact tool output): their rows stay Unverified, and the encoding is never
//! changed for them. The memory test module replays the rejected encodings
//! and records their outcomes. Private slots need no SMT memory at all:
//! their bytes are resolved exactly by the walks.
//!
//! *Width rules for memory (from the encoder and the register allocator).*
//! `MovRM8`/`MovRM16` are `mov r8/r16, m`: they write the destination's low
//! 8/16 bits, and the bits above are a free input `u_k` EVEN when the pre-RA
//! destination held a defined value. The encoder's instruction keeps those
//! bits, but register allocation treats a narrow load's destination as a
//! pure definition (`first_operand_is_def_and_use` in the x86-64 pipeline)
//! and may give it a register that holds anything, so the theorem assumes no
//! merge: a narrow load is never zero-extending and never a merge.
//! `MovzxRM8`/`MovzxRM16` are `movzx r32, m8/m16` (0F B6 / 0F B7): the
//! hardware writes the whole register with the loaded bytes zero-extended, so
//! the destination is exactly `zext(bytes)` with no free input and no merge.
//! `MovRM32` (`mov r32, m`) zero-extends into bits 32..63; `MovRM` writes 64
//! bits.
//! `MovMR8/16/32` and `MovMR` store the low 1/2/4/8 bytes of their register,
//! whose operand must name at least that many bits: register allocation keeps
//! only the low 32 bits of a 32-bit register (it spills and reloads four
//! bytes and coalesces a 32-bit copy), so an 8-byte store of a 32-bit
//! register operand is outside the domain. `[base + disp]` operands over a
//! 64-bit register or a stack slot are admitted, and (slice 25) the nine
//! scaled-index loads and stores (`MovRMSib`, `MovRM32Sib`, `MovRM8Sib`,
//! `MovzxRM8Sib`, `MovsdRMSib`, `MovssRMSib`, `MovMRSib`, `MovMR32Sib`,
//! `MovMR8Sib`) at their base-only sibling's width and extension (the encoder
//! emits the sibling's opcode byte and REX rule with a SIB ModRM), over a
//! virtual 64-bit register or stack-slot base, a virtual 64-bit index and a
//! virtual value register; the volatile forms are outside the domain.
//!
//! **Direct calls (slice 3).** A callee is modelled as an uninterpreted
//! function of a world token and its arguments, returning its result and the
//! next world, so calls happen in a fixed order. The theorem states that
//! model in its Ackermann-reduced form: the walk pairs the `c`-th machine
//! `Call` with the `c`-th source `Call` (same position, same callee symbol,
//! same exact-module binding, the System V argument and result registers the
//! source signature implies, checked in Rust, else
//! [`X86BodyMismatch::CallTarget`]/[`X86BodyMismatch::CallMetadata`]/
//! [`X86BodyMismatch::CallCount`]); both sides then read that call's result
//! as the one free input `r_c`, and the observable carries every call's
//! argument values. By induction over the calls, a Valid theorem means each
//! paired call receives the same arguments in the same world on both sides,
//! so it returns the same result and leaves the same next world: sharing
//! `r_c` is exactly the UF congruence, and an argument or order difference
//! is refuted rather than assumed. (The UF + world form itself, with the
//! argument and order checks left to the solver, did not certify on the
//! qualification host when the encoding was chosen, while the reduced form
//! did: the host-qualification test replays the UF + world form of every
//! call shape, and toolchain AY 0.27.0 build 10530 rejects its own proof of
//! each one with an argument, `strict UNSAT proof validation failed: step t1
//! uses unverified trust rule`, and leaves one unproved step in the
//! no-argument wrapper's. AY builds 12713 and 13556 certify all but the
//! `bool` argument's; the reduced form stays, since an encoding is never
//! chosen per AY. Callee identity and
//! call order are therefore established by the trusted Rust pairing, not by
//! the replayed proof; see **Trusted**.) A call clobbers every System V
//! caller-saved root (RAX RCX RDX RSI RDI R8-R11): reading one afterwards is
//! [`X86BodyMismatch::UndefinedRead`], except RAX's result bits. A callee
//! preserves RBX, RBP, R12-R15 and RSP (the walk refuses a body that writes
//! them) and cannot see virtual registers. A callee's I8/I16/I32 result defines
//! only AL/AX/EAX, and a `B1` result only AL, 0 or 1 (the psABI rule; an
//! LLVM callee returns a `bool` with `setcc %al`, leaving EAX bits 8..31 as
//! they were): the bits above are `g_c`. The backend's clean 32-bit bool is
//! a promise about its own results, so a caller must re-extend a `bool` call
//! result from AL before it returns or forwards it (and mask a `bool` lane of
//! a two-result call, see **Scalar pairs**). A body whose last call
//! never returns (`Call; Trap`, machine `CALL; UD2`) observes that call's
//! arguments and no result. A `B1` argument is observed as the backend's
//! clean 32-bit bool; a function that takes a `B1` parameter and passes a
//! `B1` argument was outside the domain until slice 15 (what the forwarded
//! register holds depends on which caller convention supplied it; the
//! argument is now observed as the clean bool under every caller view).
//! Only register
//! arguments and scalar results are admitted (and, slice 7, a private slot's
//! address as an argument, a return buffer among them, and, slice 10, a call
//! through a register): no stack arguments, varargs, invokes, aggregate or
//! i128 values. A body with no
//! result and no call argument has no observable
//! ([`X86BodyUnsupported::NoObservable`]): the `#[track_caller]` panic
//! helpers that `panic=abort` lowers to `abort()` with a dead hidden
//! location parameter are such bodies.
//!
//! **What binds the two sides.** Nothing but the verify-owned System V
//! argument-register table and ABI width rules. The source side is built from
//! the LIR alone and the machine side from the actual operands of every owned
//! instruction, so a wrong opcode, register, width, extension, argument
//! register, immediate or copy source changes the observed return bits, and
//! the replayed query becomes satisfiable. No selector value map, TV-1
//! provenance stamp, registry theorem, sampled verdict or codegen operand/ABI
//! table participates.
//!
//! **The observable is the return bits every caller the backend meets
//! reads.** A caller of an `I64` (pointer) result reads RAX; `I32` reads EAX;
//! `I16` AX; `I8` AL. That is the backend's own contract for its callers
//! (`x86_call_result_defined_bits` in the x86-64 selector: an I8/I16 call
//! result defines 8/16 bits and is copied with MOVZX from AL/AX) and what an
//! LLVM-compiled caller relies on (measured on this host, trustc stage-1 with
//! LLVM 22.1.2 for x86_64-apple-darwin at -O3: callers of
//! `-> u8/i8/u16/i16/u32/i32` re-extend from AL/AX/EAX with `movzbl %al`/
//! `movsbq %al`/`movzwl %ax`/`movswq %ax`/`movl %eax,%eax`/`cltq`).
//!
//! A `B1` (Rust `bool`) result is EAX holding 0 or 1. The System V psABI
//! (§3.2.3, "bit 0 contains the truth value and bits 1 to 7 shall be zero")
//! and an LLVM caller (`movzbl %al, %eax`, no mask) read only AL, but the
//! backend's own B1 contract is a clean 32-bit boolean: the width its own
//! results are held to and the width it passes a B1 argument at, which an
//! LLVM callee of a `bool` argument relies on (`fn(b: bool) -> u32 { b as
//! u32 }` is `movl %edi, %eax`, stage-1 rustc -O). A lowering that leaves EAX
//! bits 8..31 set would satisfy the psABI and still break a caller that
//! forwards EAX to such a callee, so the theorem observes all 32 bits. (A
//! trust-cg caller does not rely on that of ANOTHER function's `bool` result:
//! it re-extends the result from AL, see Direct calls above.)
//!
//! **Residual (documented, not checked).** LLVM's Darwin callee convention
//! also extends `i8`/`i16` return values to 32 bits for legacy callers that
//! relied on clang's old behaviour (`getTypeForExtReturn`, PR26665). The
//! backend does not promise that (the selector's narrow-return contract
//! leaves bits above AL/AX undefined; a `u8` add returns its 32-bit carrier
//! sum), and the theorem does not require it: a legacy or hand-written Darwin
//! C caller that reads EAX of a trust-cg `extern "C"` narrow-return function
//! is outside what this theorem covers. Modern LLVM and clang callers
//! re-extend.
//!
//! **Which argument bits are defined.** Only the low `W` bits of an argument
//! register hold an `I8`/`I16`/`I32`/`I64` parameter: rustc's Rust ABI marks
//! no extension on them (LLVM IR `i8 noundef`, measured) and an LLVM caller
//! passes them unextended (`pass_u8` tail-jumps with the caller's RDI). A
//! `B1` parameter has bit 0 as its value, and callers differ above it: a
//! psABI caller clears only bits 1..7, while the backend's own callers and
//! LLVM callers (`zeroext i1`) pass a clean 32-bit bool. Each view is
//! definitional (`a_i`, `z_i`); everything above the bits it clears is an
//! unconstrained entry variable. The two conventions differ only for a
//! function that both takes and returns a `B1`, which is walked under each:
//! its observable is EAX under the clean-bool views (`n_k`, what a trust-cg
//! or LLVM caller passes and reads) and AL under the psABI views (`m_k`, what
//! a psABI caller passes and reads). Every other
//! result is read identically by both caller classes, so one walk under the
//! weaker psABI views states it for both. The theorem therefore holds for
//! every caller of either class and assumes no caller's extension that the
//! caller does not provide.
//!
//! **Pointers.** LIR carries every thin pointer as an `I64`, and the trust-ir
//! adapter lowers exactly the pointer-width `PtrToPtr`/`PtrToInt`/`IntToPtr`
//! casts (and code-pointer `ReifyFnPointer`) to a LIR `Copy`. That is how the
//! owner tells a genuine no-op cast from a cast with semantics: its subject
//! is the LIR, where a `Copy` is by definition the identity at one type, a
//! `Bitcast` whose result carrier equals its operand's (a signedness
//! reinterpretation such as `i8 as u8`) is the identity too, and every cast
//! that changes bits is a distinct opcode (`Uextend`, `Sextend`, `Trunc`, a
//! carrier-changing `Bitcast`, float conversions) that the owner either
//! models exactly or refuses. A `Copy` whose result is hinted at another type
//! than its operand is refused. Aggregate pointee metadata
//! (`param_pointee_types`) describes the memory behind a pointer parameter,
//! which the theorem models by address alone (slice 4: incoming memory is
//! untyped bytes), so a pointer parameter carrying it is still its 64
//! argument bits. The trust-ir-to-LIR cast mapping itself is the adapter's,
//! outside this theorem, as it was in slice 1.
//!
//! **Width rules (verify-owned, from the encoder).** A 32-bit register write
//! zero-extends into bits 32..63 (`MovRR32`, and every 32-bit ALU form); a
//! `MovRR`/ALU row is 32-bit exactly when all its register operands are 32-bit
//! (`encode_alu_rr` clears REX.W only then) and 64-bit when all are 64-bit;
//! a row of mixed widths (slice 15) defines only its low 32 bits.
//! `Movzx`/`MovzxW`/`MovsxB`/`MovsxW`/
//! `Movsx` always carry REX.W and write the full 64-bit extension of the low
//! 8/16/32 source bits. A `MovRI` to a 32-bit destination holds the low 32
//! immediate bits zero-extended. ALU immediates are sign-extended `imm32`s;
//! shift immediates are masked to 5 (32-bit) or 6 (64-bit) bits. `Lea`/`LeaSib`
//! always compute 64 bits: `base + index*scale + disp`, wrapping, with the
//! `*1` and `+0` terms omitted for every LEA; a scaled-index access (slice
//! 25) reaches the address `LeaSib` computes from the same operand.
//!
//! **What the solver does NOT judge: preservation.** The machine walk refuses
//! any write to RBX, RBP, R12-R15 or RSP ([`X86BodyMismatch::PreservedWrite`])
//! before a theorem exists, so callee-saved and stack-pointer preservation
//! rests on that trusted Rust walk, not on the replayed proof, and the
//! theorem's observable carries only the return bits. (Slice 1 appended the
//! seven preserved entry variables, identical on both sides, to the
//! observable. Host qualification on 2026-09-27 — toolchain AY 0.27.0 build
//! 10530 with Clean 5f09e36c — measured that tail making AY's strict
//! certification reject every narrow extract/zero-extend theorem and every
//! commuted operand, "step t1 uses unverified trust rule", while the same
//! theorems without it certify, and slice 1's shapes certify either way. That
//! measurement is on the owner-admitted toolchain AY; an older AY that needed
//! the tail would leave slice-1 bodies Unverified, never credited. With AY
//! builds 12713 and 13556 the narrow theorem certifies with the tail and the
//! commuted one keeps a trusted step. The one
//! canonical encoding therefore states the return bits alone, for every
//! function; it is never chosen per function.)
//!
//! **Applicability.** The LIR source alone decides it: an acyclic CFG
//! ([`source_cfg`], slice 6; over its DAG past the slice-6 bounds, slice 9;
//! a bounded unconditional chain before slice 6), or (slice 12) a reducible
//! CFG with loops over its regions, over
//! `B1`/`I8`/`I16`/`I32`/`I64` values — constants, copies, same-carrier bitcasts,
//! wrapping add/sub/mul, and/or/xor, shifts left, logical right and
//! arithmetic right by an in-range constant, zero and sign extension,
//! truncation, direct calls with 0-6 such register arguments and at most
//! two such results, (slice 4) private stack slot addresses with constant
//! offsets (`Iadd` of a constant, `StructGep`; OD-185: `ArrayGep` at a
//! constant index) and scalar loads and stores
//! through them or through any other address, and (slice 5) symbol
//! addresses (`GlobalRef`, `ExternRef`) and branch-free compares (`Icmp`),
//! and (slice 6) branches (`Brif`, `Switch`), selects on a nonzero test and
//! reads of private bytes a path did not write, and (slice 7) private slot
//! addresses passed to direct calls, return-buffer callees among them, and
//! (slice 8) aggregates of INTEGER eightbytes as parameters, a result,
//! by-value arguments and call results, and (slice 10) checked add, subtract
//! and multiply of `I64`, bitwise not, negation, population count and indirect
//! calls, and shifts by a variable amount, and (slice 11) `I128` values in
//! register pairs and float bit patterns in SSE registers, and (slice 13)
//! private slot addresses stored as data, the C memory routines and scalar
//! atomic loads and stores, and (slice 15) float constants, `Fneg` and
//! `Fabs`, a forwarded `bool` parameter and incoming accesses through an
//! address that may derive from an escaped slot — parameters in at most six
//! integer and eight SSE argument registers (slice 13: integer parameters
//! and arguments past the integer ones on the stack; slice 15: an `I128`
//! there too) and one or two scalar results or one aggregate
//! or `I128` result (or,
//! with a call, an incoming-memory access or several paths, none; each path
//! ending in `Return` or `Trap`; or a leading sret buffer), with no slot
//! address returned or computed with (slice 13: stored as data, by an
//! 8-byte store), and no runtime-sized slot,
//! EH, indirect return-buffer callee, floating-point operation (but the
//! slice-15 ones) or MEMORY-class value, and (slice 14) division and
//! remainder of an `I8`/`I16`/`I32`/`I64`, the divide-by-zero guard, a
//! `Switch` with shared targets and its jump-table dispatch. The calling
//! convention must be supplied and must be System V. Only then is the machine
//! inspected.
//!
//! **Admission never depends on the replayer.** What the admitted AY/Clean
//! pair can certify decides only whether a built theorem earns credit, never
//! whether it is built: a shape whose theorem does not certify (on the
//! qualification host: an arithmetic shift right, a scaled LEA, a multiply
//! on a 32-bit carrier and a commuted 64-bit multiply) is inside the domain
//! with exact semantics, its rows stay Unverified
//! ([`X86_BODY_NEEDS_REPLAY`]), and a wrong lowering of it is still refuted.
//! The host-qualification test records each such outcome with the exact
//! AY/Clean output.
//!
//! **Refusals.** [`X86BodyRefusal::Unsupported`] makes no claim and leaves
//! today's uncredited rows. [`X86BodyRefusal::Mismatch`] is a recognized shape
//! that is wrong: an undefined read (including a caller-saved register read
//! after a call that does not define it, and a `setcc`, `jcc` or `cmovcc` of
//! undefined flags), a preserved-register write, a control skeleton that
//! disagrees with its own successors, layout or source end (slice 6: or with
//! the source path it is paired with, or that lays out a block no source
//! path reaches), a call that differs from its paired
//! source call in callee, binding or System V register metadata, a different
//! number of calls, a machine access outside its private stack slot, a
//! symbol address the source never takes in that form, or a returned value
//! (per result lane), call argument,
//! incoming-memory address or stored value that an exact affine evaluation
//! of both streams shows to differ (a difference that may be only a
//! reordering of the source's accesses, as under Memory above, makes no
//! claim instead). For values, call arguments and incoming memory
//! that evaluation can only REFUSE; credit comes only from independent replay
//! of the theorem (AY refutes, Clean/Carcara checks the Alethe proof). For a
//! PRIVATE stack address it decides: a frame address is not an SMT term, so
//! which slot bytes a load or store touches is computed by the walks alone,
//! from the slot index and a constant offset (see **Trusted**).
//!
//! **Non-vacuity.** Every premise has the form `(= fresh expr)`, where `fresh`
//! is declared once, never a free input (an entry register, a call's
//! `r_c`/`g_c`, a load's `l_j`, a frame byte's `f_k` or an undefined value's
//! `u_k`/`w_k`), and defined before any use. The premise set is therefore
//! satisfiable for every assignment of the free inputs. The
//! constructor checks this structurally on the built obligation and refuses
//! construction when it fails, so a builder bug cannot mint an UNSAT from
//! contradictory premises.
//!
//! **Trusted.** The two translators in this file (LIR semantics and the width
//! rules above), the System V argument, return and callee-saved tables, the
//! psABI `_Bool` rule and the backend's clean 32-bit bool rule, the walk's
//! refusal of preserved-register writes and the SMT-LIB serializer. For
//! calls: the System V caller-saved table and callee contract (a callee
//! depends only on its register arguments and the world, preserves the
//! callee-saved roots and RSP, defines only its result's ABI bits, and
//! returns a `B1` as 0/1 in AL), and the walk's positional pairing of calls
//! by callee symbol and binding (callee identity and call order are checked
//! in Rust, not by the solver). For memory: the binding of machine stack
//! slots to source slots by index, the exact byte model of private slots
//! (their disjointness from each other and from incoming memory, and the
//! escape refusal that keeps them private until a call receives one), the
//! memory width rules above,
//! the positional pairing of incoming accesses by kind and width (the
//! solver sees only the shared `l_j` and the observed addresses and values),
//! the constant folding that computes a private slot offset on each side
//! (the source's `fold_constant` over `Iconst`s and integer operations of
//! them; the machine's `lea`/`add`/`sub` of a frame address and its 64-bit
//! `imul`/`and`/`or`/`xor`/`shl` folds of constants), which decides the slot
//! bytes an access touches without the solver, the callee contract extended
//! to memory (a callee reads and writes only incoming memory, never a private
//! slot whose address did not escape), the verify-owned C layout of
//! `StructGep` and (OD-185) of the `ArrayGep` stride, and the System V sret
//! return rule. For slice 5: the System V
//! return-register table (RAX, then RDX), the rule that a `B1` lane of a
//! two-result call defines bit 0 of AL or DL alone, which holds its value
//! (the weaker of the psABI `_Bool` byte and rustc's any-extended `{ T, i1 }`
//! lane), the shared x86 EFLAGS model of `cmp` and its condition evaluation
//! (`x86_64_eflags`), the rule that every
//! other admitted ALU row rewrites the flags and a call leaves them
//! undefined, and the symbol binding: a `LeaRip` of a symbol is the address
//! the source's `GlobalRef` names and a `MovRipRel` the address its
//! `ExternRef` names (the relocation encodings and the loader's filling of
//! the GOT slot are the encoder's, linker's and loader's, as the per-row
//! relocation proofs state, not this theorem's). For slice 6: the path
//! enumeration, the machine walk's control semantics (a taken `jcc`
//! continues at its target, an untaken one at the next row, a block without
//! a final transfer at the next block in `block_order`; the pairing by
//! provenance and the successor and fallthrough rules above only choose
//! which machine path a source path is judged against, never what it
//! computes), the EFLAGS model of `test`, the
//! rule that flags reach only readers of the block that wrote them, the
//! `cmovcc` rule (a tied destination), the admitted `Switch` constants'
//! meaning (the adapter's normalized pattern of the selector's width), and
//! the frame-byte rule (a private byte read before any store on the path is
//! the slot's initial value on both sides). For slice 7: the callee contract
//! extended to escaped slots (a callee reads and writes the bytes of the
//! slots whose address escaped to it or to an earlier call, and of no other
//! slot; its result, next world and the bytes it leaves there depend only
//! on its register arguments, the world and those bytes), the walk's check
//! that a machine argument passes exactly the paired private address (a
//! private address is no SMT term), and the provenance rule behind the
//! retention rule (an address computed only from parameters, symbols,
//! constants and what loads and calls produced before any slot escaped
//! cannot point into a slot of this frame, so an access through it observes
//! no slot). For slice 8: the System V
//! INTEGER-eightbyte classification of an admitted aggregate (the
//! verify-owned [`classify_aggregate`] and its C layout; a unit test sweeps
//! it against the selector's real output, and the walk compares every call
//! row's declared registers and widths with it), the callee contract for an
//! aggregate result (the callee defines each eightbyte's bytes in RAX or RDX
//! and nothing above them; the caller reads those bytes and no more), the
//! LIR meaning of an aggregate-typed value (the bytes at its address when it
//! is passed or returned; the bridge's mapping of a Rust argument's
//! `PassMode` to that LIR type is the bridge's, outside this theorem), the
//! tied two-operand rule (`d = d op x` for `shl`, `shr` and `or`) and the
//! narrow physical alias rule (a byte or word load into AL..R15B or AX..R15W
//! defines its low bits and leaves the bits above a free input). For slice
//! 9: the topological admission and walk (a block starts from its one
//! predecessor's state on the edge into it, or from the merge of its
//! incoming edges' states at a join; a machine block is walked once per
//! outgoing source edge over the rows [`block_rows`] pairs with it), the
//! merge rules ([`merge_admitted`], [`merge_machine`]: what a join keeps,
//! and every value, register and private byte the edges leave differently
//! chosen by the source edges' guards), the guard definitions and the
//! partition of a block's runs by its edges' tests, which together make the
//! DAG theorem equivalent to the per-path conjunction (the slice-9
//! equivalence test samples both on every fixture). For slice
//! 10: the trust-ir meaning of checked arithmetic, `Not` and `CtPop` as
//! stated above and the stated refinement of an out-of-range variable shift,
//! the Intel SDM count masking of the shifts by CL (and that the count is
//! the implicit RCX), the Intel SDM flag semantics of `add`, `sub`, `imul` and
//! `mul` (and the rule that their undefined flags are never read), the
//! implicit RAX/RDX operands and widths of `mul`, the one statement of
//! population count and of a shift by an amount modulo the width that both
//! sides use ([`count_ones`], [`shift_modulo_width`]), the tied `and d, imm`
//! rule, the pairing of indirect calls with `call r64` and the callee
//! contract for an indirect callee. For slice 11: the System V register
//! classes and assignment of `I128` and float values ([`argument_roots`],
//! [`result_roots`]: two consecutive INTEGER registers for an `I128`, the
//! SSE registers numbered apart for floats; a unit test sweeps them against
//! the selector's real output, and the walk compares every call row's
//! declared registers with them), the callee contract for an `I128` result
//! (it defines all of RAX and RDX) and a float result (it defines the low
//! 32 or 64 bits of XMM0 or XMM1, nothing above) and for the SSE registers
//! (a call may change every one), the LIR meaning of `I128` constants and
//! the split of a 16-byte incoming access into two 8-byte ones, the Intel
//! SDM semantics of `adc`, `sbb`, the one-operand shifts by CL and the SSE
//! moves admitted (and the register allocator's treatment of a `movss`
//! destination as a pure definition), and that a float `Bitcast` keeps the
//! bits. For slice 12: the natural-loop analysis (dominators, reducibility,
//! the regions and their cut edges), the meaning of a header's edge copies
//! (run in order before the `Jump`, a temporary only they read), the
//! fixpoint checks that what a region assumes from its entry edge is
//! unchanged on every edge into the header (a source value defined once; a
//! machine register or private byte compared term for term), the claim that
//! a run between two visits of a header runs only its loop's blocks, and the
//! escape over-approximation at a join and a header (a callee that could not
//! reach a slot leaves its bytes unchanged). The pairing of a parameter with
//! a register (the TV-1 stamps) and the bits the relation states only
//! choose what the theorem checks, never what it assumes.
//! For slice 13: the transitive escape rule ([`escape_source`],
//! [`escape_machine`]: whoever can read an escaped slot can follow an address
//! stored in it), the frame's placement of the `k`-th incoming stack
//! eightbyte at `[rbp + 16 + 8k]` and of the callee's `k`-th stack argument
//! at `[rsp + 8k]` at the call (the verify-owned [`parameter_locations`] and
//! [`stack_arguments`]; a unit test sweeps the former against the selector's
//! formal-argument bindings), that the symbols `memcpy`, `memmove` and
//! `memset` are the C runtime's routines (whose effect is the intrinsic's),
//! bound outside the link unit under proof (OD-217 refuses a definition of
//! the module's, its link unit's, or the function's own), and their callee
//! contract, and trust-ir's single-threaded meaning of the
//! admitted atomics (OD-83). For slice 15: trust-ir's meanings of a float
//! constant and of `Fneg`/`Fabs` as stated (a unit test checks them against
//! the pinned interpreter), that a constant-pool entry is emitted read-only
//! with its bytes at the address its RIP-relative load resolves to (the
//! encoder's and linker's, as for a symbol), the Intel SDM semantics of
//! `movss`/`movsd` from memory and of `pxor`/`andps`/`andpd`, the encoder's
//! width rule for a row of mixed register widths (only its low 32 bits are
//! relied on), the extended callee contract for an access through a derived
//! address (it reads or writes incoming memory and the escaped slots' bytes
//! only, as a call may), and the psABI placement of an `I128` in the stack
//! argument areas (two 16-byte-aligned eightbytes; a unit test sweeps it
//! against the selector). For slice 14: trust-ir's meaning of division and
//! where it is undefined as stated (a unit test checks both against the
//! pinned interpreter), the LIR meaning of `GuardDivZero` (a trap exactly
//! when its divisor is zero), the Intel SDM semantics of `div`, `idiv`,
//! `cqo`, `cdq` and of `xor r, r, r` (and that the double-width dividend's
//! simplification when its high half is that zero or that sign is exact, a
//! unit test sweeps both forms against a direct computation), the
//! exactness of the division spellings (a signed division's unit-divisor
//! cases and the power-of-two shifts, swept by unit tests; the pairing's
//! restatement holds by construction, [`pair_divisions`]), the
//! pipeline's expansion of a `TrapDivZeroExact` carrier to `test d, d; je ->
//! ud2` at the register's width after this theorem's subject (checked by its
//! guard-carrier canary, outside this theorem, like the `popcnt` expansion),
//! the stop bookkeeping that waives a claim after an undefined division and
//! orders a trap before a fault ([`UnitStops`], [`stop_claims`]), the
//! adapter's copy blocks that leave a shared `Switch` target without
//! arguments, the routes of a compare chain ([`block_routes`]), and the
//! jump-table dispatch: that the codegen emits the table into the
//! function's `.text` after its body with entry `i` the offset of
//! `targets[i]`'s block from the table, that the RIP-relative `lea` resolves
//! to the table, and the walk's recognition of the dispatch idiom
//! ([`dispatch_blocks`]). For slice 21 (OD-198 to OD-201): the Intel SDM
//! transcription of the SSE rounding rows (each computes the IEEE 754
//! operation its [`machine_float_key`] names, its result a function of its
//! operands' patterns; `cvttsd2si` returns the integer indefinite outside the
//! range; `ucomisd`'s flags as stated), that MXCSR holds its default control
//! state (`0x1F80`: round to nearest even, no flush-to-zero, no
//! denormals-are-zero, exceptions masked) whenever a body runs, trust-ir's
//! meaning of the admitted floating-point operations (the IEEE 754
//! operation at the operand's format, round to nearest even, any NaN; the
//! saturating `FcvtToInt`), that the frame saves and restores RBX around a
//! body with the `cmpxchg16b` pseudo, that the 16 bytes an `I128` atomic
//! load reads are writable, and the single-threaded meaning of the two
//! atomic pseudo rows (the CAS loop runs once). For slice 22 (OD-202): that
//! the routine the link binds to `__udivti3`, `__divti3`, `__umodti3` or
//! `__modti3`, a definition outside the module under proof and its link
//! unit (OD-210 refuses the module's own, OD-216 the link unit's), returns
//! trust-ir's 128-bit `udiv`, `sdiv`, `urem` or
//! `srem` of its two System V `I128` arguments on trust-ir's defined domain
//! (tested, never proved: a native differential against an independent
//! bit-serial reference), and (OD-211, more than the callee contract above,
//! which lets a callee rewrite the escaped slots and the world) that it
//! writes no memory the caller observes, has no effect on the world and
//! returns. Slice 23 (OD-205, OD-206) trusts nothing new: every consumer
//! re-derives a paired theorem's pairing from its pairing record
//! ([`X86BodyPairing::check`]; what stays trusted is the walk's and the
//! spelling's, as before), and a generalization credits only by
//! instantiation and every consumer re-derives it.
//! The walk
//! gives no pre-RA
//! row a meaning the register allocator does not keep: a narrow load's or a
//! `setcc`'s destination and a 32-bit register's bits above 32 are never
//! relied on (see the memory width rules). The subject is the pre-RA ISel stream:
//! register allocation (its moves on control-flow edges included), the
//! two-address fixup, the frame, the call's stack alignment and encoding
//! (branch displacement resolution and the JCC-erratum padding among it)
//! are covered only by the TV-4/TV-5/ENC-3 validators, not by this theorem.
//! The optimizer passes that reshape control flow (branch layout, block
//! merging, `cmov` if-conversion at O1+) run before it: the pairing judges
//! their output.

